WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:15.540
SANTOS: I'm Adele Santos, Dean
of the School of architecture

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.100
and Planning here
at MIT, and it's

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.300
my pleasure to welcome
you all on this hideous

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.380
evening, actually.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.600
It's great that so
many of you came,

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.390
and I want to thank Mark
[INAUDIBLE] who is, I think,

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:33.680
here, and Darian [INAUDIBLE],,
wherever he is sitting.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:34.809
There you go, Darian.

00:00:34.809 --> 00:00:35.850
For doing this, actually.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.610
For coming with the idea, for
doing the research [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.430
and making this event happen.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:46.090
We're very proud that
[INAUDIBLE] at our school.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.885
Just a few words about
MIT and the kinds

00:00:48.885 --> 00:00:51.660
of issues that are going
to be discussed today.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.750
MIT takes on-- when
problems are presented,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.207
it often takes on the
issues with great speed

00:00:59.207 --> 00:01:04.844
and helpfulness
[INAUDIBLE] for example.

00:01:04.844 --> 00:01:08.650
It went out of its way to
deal with issues of equity,

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.505
and I think from
that period onward,

00:01:10.505 --> 00:01:14.970
the whole issue of increasing
diversity on our campus

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:20.040
has been something that's
constantly discussed.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.770
It may seem that
we are rarefied,

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:27.578
but the truth is we've looked
at the numbers of students

00:01:27.578 --> 00:01:29.202
from all over the
world, from all sorts

00:01:29.202 --> 00:01:31.580
of different ethnicities
increasing by year.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.260
It's improving from the number
of black students and Latino

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.140
students.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.826
We've also been making
an equitably large effort

00:01:38.826 --> 00:01:41.760
to try and bring in a
more diverse faculty.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.080
The truth is we do bring
in quite large numbers

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.300
of black faculty
in some disciplines

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.418
but we do a lousy job of somehow
keeping them, and getting them

00:01:52.418 --> 00:01:55.130
through the process to
tenure, and this is something

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.270
we're very acutely aware of.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.955
I've been here for
three years now,

00:02:00.955 --> 00:02:04.190
and we have somebody sitting
on academic council that

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.223
meets every week who
actually is in charge of some

00:02:07.223 --> 00:02:08.639
of these issues,
particularly when

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.870
we say under represented
minorities, which really,

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.270
includes women still
today, although I think

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:19.960
the issues that have been
problematic for women have been

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.420
on the process of being solved.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.350
We, in our school,
also have a committee

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.030
that deals with issues of
diversity of which Mark

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.230
has been a lead player
for quite a time,

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:37.760
and we are the first school
at MIT to have hired somebody

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.458
who's full-time job is to
be the manager of diversity

00:02:41.458 --> 00:02:41.958
recruitment.

00:02:41.958 --> 00:02:43.870
And where are you?

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.380
Could you please stand up?

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.732
This is Robin Chatwin, who is--

00:02:47.732 --> 00:02:51.676
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:51.676 --> 00:02:57.610
Robin is charged with
helping us get the best

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:01.120
database we can in the world.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.060
She's here to work with
our diversity committee.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.300
She's here to work with
the student committees,

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.293
and I felt that if we
went on talking about it,

00:03:11.293 --> 00:03:14.285
and didn't actually have
someway to be looked

00:03:14.285 --> 00:03:17.282
after on a day to day basis,
we weren't doing as good a job

00:03:17.282 --> 00:03:18.220
as we can.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.410
So Robin, we were very
fortunate to find her.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:29.080
She's got her PhD from MIT,
and EECS by the media lab.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:29.950
So she knew MIT.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.880
She knew our culture, and
bless her, she put up her hand,

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.852
and said I'd like to do
this, and here she is.

00:03:37.852 --> 00:03:40.640
And I've heard you'll have
a chance to speak with her,

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:45.900
and I'm sure she'll be asking
your help in all sorts of ways.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.108
So that's Robin.

00:03:48.108 --> 00:03:53.353
So I would like to now turn
to welcoming Ellen Weiss, who

00:03:53.353 --> 00:03:59.053
received her PhD from the
University of Illinois Urbana

00:03:59.053 --> 00:04:02.290
Champagne.

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:04.680
And she's now a professor
of architectural history

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.796
today at the University, and
somehow survived the flood,

00:04:07.796 --> 00:04:12.360
and is there actively trying
to keep the school together

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.635
[INAUDIBLE].

00:04:13.635 --> 00:04:15.930
She has received many
awards for her work,

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:22.140
and she studies the history of
African American architects.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.670
One of the people
she focuses in on

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.380
is Robert R. Taylor, who
graduated from MIT very early

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:30.186
on.

00:04:30.186 --> 00:04:33.850
He was the first black
architect to graduate

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.235
from this degree
in architecture,

00:04:36.235 --> 00:04:38.535
who went on then to
have a glorious career,

00:04:38.535 --> 00:04:42.596
and was very influential in
the building of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:42.596 --> 00:04:46.119
So we thought it was appropriate
to start with you, Ellen.

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:48.793
And the drawings,
as you probably all

00:04:48.793 --> 00:04:51.938
realize now are
from Taylor's thesis

00:04:51.938 --> 00:04:56.520
we have in our collection, and
it's outside the [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:57.990
really quite interesting.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:02.385
So with that introduction,
welcome, and thank you.

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:04.357
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:26.542 --> 00:05:29.590
WEISS: Dean Santos, the
news is better than that.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.730
Robert Taylor is MIT's
first black graduate,

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:35.422
not just the school
of architecture,

00:05:35.422 --> 00:05:38.970
and he's also the first
professional trained

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.605
African-American
architect, and was

00:05:40.605 --> 00:05:43.045
the first who was trained in
the school [INAUDIBLE] when

00:05:43.045 --> 00:05:44.270
I was trained.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:48.085
I think that's very significant,
and quite wonderful.

00:05:48.085 --> 00:05:52.344
I'm very grateful to MIT
for inviting me here.

00:05:52.344 --> 00:05:56.670
I want very, very much
for MIT to realize

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.672
the historical gem you have.

00:05:59.672 --> 00:06:02.152
[AUDIO OUT]

00:06:17.591 --> 00:06:19.590
Robert Taylor was the
first academically trained

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:24.590
African-American architect,
and MIT's first black graduate,

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:27.050
class of 1892.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:30.560
He spent his career as a
teacher, administrator,

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.300
and designer of Booker T.
Washington's Tuskegee Normal

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.480
and Industrial Institute, a
residential vocational high

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.750
school in east central Alabama.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:47.150
He designed around 40 brick
buildings there, and elsewhere,

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:50.840
plus some unknown
number of wooden ones.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.606
It was Taylor's good fortune--

00:06:53.606 --> 00:06:57.130
I completely forgot
they come with slides.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.776
It's my responsibility.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.132
Is it on?

00:07:09.132 --> 00:07:09.840
Yes, there we go.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:10.870
There he is.

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:15.480
There he is in 1906.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.210
It was Taylor's
good fortune to be

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.520
born in Wilmington,
North Carolina, which

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.340
was a prosperous port town
with, before the civil war,

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:28.380
many, many excellent black
builders, and some builder

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:32.490
architects, both slave and free.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:34.920
During Reconstruction,
Wilmington even

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.095
had black residents
on the city council.

00:07:37.095 --> 00:07:41.340
It was ideally close to
being something approaching

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.340
an integrated city, except of
course, as many of you know,

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.700
it all erupted and
reversed in 1898

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.540
with the so-called
Wilmington Massacre,

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:50.540
but we're not going there.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:52.300
Taylor wasn't
there at that time.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:57.400
Robert Taylor's father was a
literate [INAUDIBLE] slave,

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:58.350
who's own--

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:03.090
technically save-- who's own
white father and master allowed

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.310
him the personal
freedom so that he

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:10.390
could prosper as a merchant,
carpenter, and contractor.

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:12.030
This is the Taylor
family home, which

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.160
was built by his
father, Henry Taylor,

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:17.860
only now we have the
little [AUDIO OUT]

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:22.400
Robert Taylor standing on the
right in the final photographs.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:23.980
He's always on the
edge of the group,

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:27.460
and I wanted to linger
on this to point out

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:31.420
the little boy there
was Taylor's son.

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:35.179
But instead, he worked as a
builder for a year or two,

00:08:35.179 --> 00:08:37.950
and then went north to MIT.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.140
Taylor's MIT years
were a success,

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.690
as Clarence Williams has shown.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:46.270
He was at or near
the top of his class,

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:48.530
and awarded a scholarship
for the second two

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:51.370
years of his four-year course.

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:53.930
While Taylor's
architecture appears

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:59.180
[INAUDIBLE] in course four,
were distinctly Yankee,

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:04.170
his larger institute class
would have been international.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.180
A description of the
class of the previous year

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:11.020
put it this way,
raised at the edge.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:19.000
67% were white complexioned,
33% dark, 11% wore eye glasses,

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:24.943
and one was color blind, and
50% thought they had mustaches.

00:09:24.943 --> 00:09:30.790
Taylor clearly belonged in the
later 50% as well as the 33%.

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:33.030
The academic schedule
was demanding,

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.300
with too many courses
by modern standards,

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:39.030
and such particular
architecture course for horrors

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:41.883
is called [INAUDIBLE]
drawings [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:41.883 --> 00:09:45.760
done on the blackboard to
then unspecified scale.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.580
Course four had an
art based pedagogy,

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.670
with history
lectures with paired

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:56.140
slides, weekly supervised trips
to the Museum of Fine Arts,

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:58.385
prestigious research
in the library,

00:09:58.385 --> 00:10:02.560
monthly designed problems,
and juried reviews.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.822
As one student wag put it,
where is an all well-conducted

00:10:06.822 --> 00:10:10.390
abattoirs, the customs is to
slaughter before rendering,

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:12.740
the process here is
reversed, and all work

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:16.420
is rendered first, and
slaughtered afterwords.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:20.775
Taylor also learned from
MIT's own construction, namely

00:10:20.775 --> 00:10:23.280
the purpose designed
architecture building

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:26.920
that was up the after he left.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:31.080
Pearce building was the six
story, industrial structure

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:34.250
with a stone and
brick mason facade

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:38.740
that freely mixes classical,
medieval, and industrial

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:40.390
elements in ways that suggest--

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:43.880
I think suggests aspects of
what Taylor would eventually

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:46.280
imply at Tuskegee.

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:47.840
Taylor's final thesis.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.520
An ideal to own during
the lifetime of practice

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:53.500
was a home for
civil war veterans

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.240
from both North and South.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.410
I need to interject a little
bit parenthetically right here.

00:11:01.779 --> 00:11:07.241
Taylor is not MIT's only
contribution to MIT,

00:11:07.241 --> 00:11:10.560
an engineering student
named Harry B. Taylor

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.635
didn't graduate,
but was probably

00:11:12.635 --> 00:11:16.700
around at about this time,
arrived at Tuskegee shortly

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:20.560
after Taylor did, and stayed
there for his entire career.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.340
This is something
that [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.090
students can take on.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:27.950
Taylor was corresponding with
Booker T. Washington somewhere

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:29.885
after graduation.

00:11:29.885 --> 00:11:31.715
While he was a, quote,
building mechanic

00:11:31.715 --> 00:11:34.900
at a hotel on Martha's
Vineyard, Washington

00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:38.020
was recruiting university
educated African-Americans

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:39.572
for Tuskegee.

00:11:39.572 --> 00:11:43.910
He also wanted a black architect
to design the expanding campus,

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:48.220
and to prove the race's
ability to a watching world.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.760
Washington with seeing
the young Richard Morris

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:55.520
Hunts, three large buildings
go up at the Hampton Institute.

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:58.630
Architects, Washington
learned, can best

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:02.340
articulate an
institution's purposes.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:05.170
Taylor then went to Cleavland
to begin a practice,

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:09.640
but soon accepted
Washington's lure, arriving

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:13.060
at Tuskegee in November 1892.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:17.000
His first Alabama completion was
the frame industry's building,

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.020
and another school,
and he probably

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.030
also drew the cottage
plans and elevations

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:24.480
that Washington
gave out to farmers

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:28.680
who attended the institute's
self-help conferences.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:31.090
Tuskegee was always
about outreach.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:34.390
It's mission, a vast
rural hinterland

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:37.000
with hundreds of
thousands of very, very

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:42.130
poor, usually landless farmers,
sharecroppers, tend farmers.

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:44.770
Brick buildings
at Tuskegee began

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:47.545
with-- it was first
called science, and then

00:12:47.545 --> 00:12:52.930
Thrasher Hall, and
then the chapel.

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:54.520
Inside the chapel,
I particularly

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:55.735
liked this photograph.

00:12:55.735 --> 00:12:58.200
It's very hard to get
photographs inside the chapel,

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:00.020
but these are not students.

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:01.170
These are the farmers.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:03.640
This is a farmers
conference, and when

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:08.110
you think about how these people
from the dregs of the bottom

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:12.909
of society were
invited into what

00:13:12.909 --> 00:13:14.700
would be the vastest
building they probably

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:18.880
could ever enter in their
lives, and the building

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:19.745
was electrically it.

00:13:19.745 --> 00:13:23.920
It was the first electrically
building in the county.

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:26.365
I think that we begin to
understand the meaning

00:13:26.365 --> 00:13:29.810
and the significance of this
young [INAUDIBLE] architect

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:32.570
makes it a [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:33.810
Chapel.

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:38.650
The Oaks, which is Booker T.
Washington's home, Boys Trades

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:42.640
Building, and Huntington Home.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:44.390
There would have
also been innumerable

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:48.460
wooden cottages, barns, rural
schools, and other service

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.050
structures.

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:53.760
Teaching, however, was
Taylor's heaviest task.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.750
There he is at the edge.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:59.320
Since students had to draw
everything they were to make,

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:03.835
every trade-- steam filling,
plumbing, shoe making, tin

00:14:03.835 --> 00:14:05.800
smithing, tailoring,
carriage building,

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:09.640
wheel routing started
with plans and elevations

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:13.390
because drawing promoted
modern industrial processes,

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:17.510
and was thought, consequently,
genuine intellectual growth.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:20.920
Drawing had a racial dimension
because it taught planning,

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:25.460
or as they thought, turned
the executive thinking

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:28.780
that slave artisans, who
supposedly worked only

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:32.580
under strict supervision,
were thought to have lacked.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:36.000
Taylor also developed
advanced drawing courses

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:40.000
for the building trades, and a
pre-professional post-graduate

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.790
architectural certificate
that he awarded

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:46.165
to [INAUDIBLE] major, the
future architect, William Sydney

00:14:46.165 --> 00:14:48.490
Pittman.

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:50.740
Tuskegee's army of
drawing students

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:53.220
probably overwhelmed
the teacher,

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.780
contributing to
his 1899 decision

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.616
to return to Cleavland.

00:14:57.616 --> 00:15:01.210
It took two instructors
to replace him.

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:03.850
In Cleveland, Taylor worked
for a white architect

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:06.140
with some Rockefeller
commissions,

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:10.540
and then on his own, but he
kept Tuskegee as a client.

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:16.485
From Cleveland, he did the
Girls Industries building,

00:15:16.485 --> 00:15:19.610
and here it is more
recently, but not now,

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:22.320
which was called Dorothy Hall.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:25.155
The Lincoln Gates, and
that's actually Dorothy Hall

00:15:25.155 --> 00:15:27.647
and the chapel behind it.

00:15:27.647 --> 00:15:29.980
In fact, they all have the
same donors-- the [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:33.840
step-sisters of New York who
gave the gates, and Dorothy

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.450
Hall, and the Chapel.

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:38.420
The boys and girls
the bath houses.

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:40.170
Here's the boys bath house.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:41.870
When you work on a
project too long,

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:45.375
and you find that it's lucky you
have a shot of it because it's

00:15:45.375 --> 00:15:49.290
since been demolished,
and Huntington Memorial

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:52.100
Academic building,
which I'm going

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:55.860
to show you in color because
tragically, that too was gone.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:57.570
It burned.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:02.700
Taylor returned to Tuskegee
around 1902, but now

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:05.520
as Director of Boy's Industries.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.880
He headed 20 to 30
trades divisions,

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:13.110
each with its own shop,
classroom, and faculty.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:15.060
As an academic
administrator, he was

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:17.640
responsible for
hundreds of students

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.860
who were enrolled in a
complicated curriculum that

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:22.980
interlocked with the
academic program,

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:25.560
and with much
greater difficulty,

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:29.100
with income-generating
industrial production.

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:32.750
Tuskegee depended on such
commercial enterprises

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:34.720
as printing
implemented [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:39.110
or brick making for income
and in Washington's theory,

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:40.750
to win intolerance.

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:45.390
We know now, of course, how
tragic such hopes turned sour

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.720
elsewhere, but Washington
and his successors

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:51.360
actually made it work
for Macon County.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.340
Tuskegee remained an oasis
of safety when other places

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:55.820
weren't.

00:16:55.820 --> 00:17:00.270
Tuskegee also projected
a quasi-Utopian image

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.700
to the northern
philanthropists that it

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:08.069
was supporting because it fed,
clothed, and sheltered itself

00:17:08.069 --> 00:17:11.609
with its own skilled hands,
while preparing the students

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:14.940
to go out, and do
the same for others.

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:18.660
Because Tuskegee constructed
its own buildings,

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:22.740
it also failed to tailor to
coordinate simultaneous jobs

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:26.130
among the various shops, seeing
to it that material, foramen,

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:29.190
and student workers
were smoothly deployed,

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:33.250
and the buildings came in
on time, and within budget.

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:36.570
He was also responsible
for campus infrastructure,

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:39.980
the endless drainage and
sanitation problems, power

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.230
and water supply,
walkways and roads,

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:46.310
and the minutia of individual
office and classroom needs--

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:48.880
hanging a picture,
or cutting a doorway,

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.650
or getting the graffiti
washed out of a bathroom.

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.475
It is hard to
imagine how he did it

00:17:54.475 --> 00:17:56.820
all while simultaneously
running all

00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:00.570
of the industrial
divisions, and after hours,

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:02.220
designing buildings.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.820
He seems to have credited
his education for his ability

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:08.760
to juggle multiple
tasks, but he also

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.930
rested by reading,
nurturing a special fondness

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:13.800
for Shakespeare.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:19.770
Taylor's architecture from 1901
until 1915, when Washington

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:22.350
died, included still
more brick buildings,

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.890
such as the Carnegie
Library, which is still alive

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:28.525
and well, at least
on the outside,

00:18:28.525 --> 00:18:34.090
not the inside, Rockefeller
Hall, the four Emory

00:18:34.090 --> 00:18:41.450
dormitories, the office
building, Douglas Hall--

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:44.150
that's an entrance
into an auditorium.

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:48.545
He started taking advantage
of the slope, the arched part

00:18:48.545 --> 00:18:49.690
and the low part.

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:52.740
Tantum Hall-- whoops.

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:54.780
Douglas Hall, White Hall.

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:56.670
This is White Hall.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:58.160
Tantum Hall.

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:01.200
I skipped Andrew Hospital
for various reasons,

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:03.200
but there is a hospital in here.

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:04.750
The veterinary hospital.

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:10.845
The veterinary hospital,
and the new laundry.

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:16.690
He also designed
Carnegie Library

00:19:16.690 --> 00:19:21.620
for Wiley University in
Marshall, Texas, and Livingston

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:24.650
college is my favorite,
so I put it last.

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:28.390
Livingston college in
Salisbury, North Carolina.

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.450
He did not design
the largest building

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:32.802
of the period,
Tompkins Dining Hall,

00:19:32.802 --> 00:19:34.260
and you're seeing
it from the rear,

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:37.050
so you can see how big
a complex this one is.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:40.170
Perhaps-- and this
is my speculation--

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:41.790
perhaps, because
he did not trust

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:45.490
himself to work at a scale
that demanded steel framing.

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:49.840
A white experienced
courthouse did it,

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:52.140
but our guy represented
the Institute

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:56.370
and the architect to each other,
shaped Washington's programming

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:59.400
and planning decisions,
argued the project

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:03.870
before the trustees, and after
the white guy's untimely death

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:06.480
with construction,
figured out how to set

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:09.260
the dome on wood trusses--

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:10.690
kind of see that
there's a dome--

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:14.310
dome on wood trusses without
having posts interrupt

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:17.700
the dining room's spacial
unity that Washington

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:18.540
very badly wanted.

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:28.870
Taylor's buildings revealed
his MIT-trained Bozart roots,

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:32.910
even when there are no obvious
classical elements on display,

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:36.210
as there are in the new Laundry.

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:37.991
They are characterized
by triadic masons--

00:20:37.991 --> 00:20:39.740
this is what you're
supposed to see here--

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:41.900
with emphasized centers
that are embraced

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:46.850
by flanking units that lend a
strong sense of horizontality

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:48.540
to the mass.

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:51.500
They have finely
tuned proportions,

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:57.660
syncopated arrangements of
window shapes and sizes.

00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:00.120
This is the stair
hall being picked out

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:02.660
from the back of a dormitory.

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:05.170
This is a case where there's
a real skin flint donor.

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:06.750
I think I have the front of it.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:08.990
Taylor wanted a
two-story broader veranda

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:13.630
that would have more Palladian
impacts, centralizing impact,

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:15.700
and the donor was too
cheap to allow it.

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:17.700
So Taylor-- let's
see if we can reverse

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:21.900
it-- got his jollies by
doing the back like that.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:28.130
Didn't cost a
cent, I would hope.

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:30.190
Occasional bursts of wit--

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:32.490
[INAUDIBLE] out of order here.

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:36.200
Those are the
syncopated windows.

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:40.270
Occasional bursts of wit,
hence the rhythmic play

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:42.046
on the second level.

00:21:42.046 --> 00:21:44.120
There's nothing that
really demands that,

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:46.105
but I think the rhythm
of the entire facade,

00:21:46.105 --> 00:21:48.320
and we can't see
the entire facade,

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:52.590
suggests that somebody very
clever is doing marvelous

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:57.080
stuff with minimal means.

00:21:57.080 --> 00:22:02.436
And contrasts between sharp-cut
window voids, and the wall

00:22:02.436 --> 00:22:07.100
planes that display
Tuskegee's richly

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:09.390
textured homemade bricks.

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:11.295
Tuskegee's bricks
are almost a kind

00:22:11.295 --> 00:22:14.150
of icon of the
institution, and I'll

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:16.970
try not to go on
and on about that.

00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:18.306
Just leave it up there.

00:22:18.306 --> 00:22:20.680
Governor Washington talks
about when you get discouraged,

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.138
think about the
first brick making.

00:22:22.138 --> 00:22:25.490
We almost failed,
but we carried on,

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:29.830
and we could build our school.

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:35.695
A few structures do break
into the formal rhetoric

00:22:35.695 --> 00:22:40.490
of projecting homes under
entablatures and pediments.

00:22:40.490 --> 00:22:43.280
Latin and Greek in
the words Washington

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:47.600
would use when you want to
put down what he considered

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.790
useless pretentiousness.

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:51.590
Washington once
told the students

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:54.310
that John D. Rockefeller,
who had given Tuskegee

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:57.720
a plain but elegantly
proportioned dormitory--

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:59.475
this is not it.

00:22:59.475 --> 00:23:01.550
I'm a little bit messed up here.

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:03.145
Who had given a plain--

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:10.600
just leave it on this.

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:12.410
A plain, but
elegantly proportioned

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:16.732
dormitory, always used words of
one syllable, rather than two,

00:23:16.732 --> 00:23:18.930
or two rather than three.

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:21.900
Rockefeller used no
Latin or Greek quotes,

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:24.530
and he was the richest
man in the world.

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:30.170
Full-blown classicism of early
20th century negro buildings

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:33.920
also raises thorny
critical questions.

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:37.750
The classical portico on
Tuskegee's Carnegie library--

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:39.680
and this is what we
want now, and here's

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:41.130
Tuskegee's Carnegie Library--

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:45.230
appeared the same year that
the new Alabama Constitution

00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:48.560
stripped most black
people of the vote.

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:50.690
Whites were using
classical imagery

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:53.810
on similar institutions,
including courthouses

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:57.690
at the same time that this was
built, and at the same time

00:23:57.690 --> 00:24:01.710
they were sanctioning violence
to enforce the increasing Jim

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:04.010
Crow restrictions running
through the South.

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:14.520
During the 1920s,
Taylor partnered

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:18.600
with a younger Carnegie-trained
black architect, Leo Persley,

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:20.750
on still more
Tuskegee buildings.

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:23.510
The [INAUDIBLE] Children's
House was the center of it

00:24:23.510 --> 00:24:24.570
right there.

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:30.930
Sage hall, James Hall, and
the quad for the new college

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:37.950
department with a larger science
building, and a new library

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:39.520
building.

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:41.340
Here, much larger
facilities, and more

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:44.670
normative, and perhaps,
less interesting to us.

00:24:44.670 --> 00:24:46.730
You have to get into
at least some of them.

00:24:46.730 --> 00:24:49.270
This is Logan Hall, which
actually combines a gym

00:24:49.270 --> 00:24:52.974
and an auditorium in one
space with a stage at the end,

00:24:52.974 --> 00:24:55.910
and underneath the stage
is as swimming pool.

00:24:55.910 --> 00:24:58.890
This became a neighborhood
cultural center with first run

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:02.330
movies, and famous musicians
who would travel through.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:05.206
The white town, white
Tuskegee, could definitely

00:25:05.206 --> 00:25:06.610
afford anything like this.

00:25:06.610 --> 00:25:10.470
Tuskegee was really triumphing
over the town itself.

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:14.000
Taylor personally also
did substantial structures

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:15.020
elsewhere.

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:18.856
This is at Selma University in
Selma, and I can't show you--

00:25:18.856 --> 00:25:20.480
and it's just as well
because it really

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:24.710
is pretty awesome--
a 64,000 square-foot,

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:30.350
seven-story colored Masonic
temple cum office building that

00:25:30.350 --> 00:25:34.460
was also the social center
of Birmingham's black elite

00:25:34.460 --> 00:25:37.560
through the age of segregation.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:39.286
Important building.

00:25:39.286 --> 00:25:41.650
I don't have a decent
photograph of it.

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:43.880
Most Taylor and
Persley buildings

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:47.390
were contracted to the
black-owned Wyndham brothers

00:25:47.390 --> 00:25:51.850
firm in Birmingham, a company
in which Taylor was invested.

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:55.430
Taylor seems to have had
inherited his father's business

00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:56.990
acumen, and had
many investments,

00:25:56.990 --> 00:26:00.320
and little businesses on the
side, about which I wish I

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:01.490
knew more.

00:26:01.490 --> 00:26:04.740
He's had five children
through college.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:08.320
Throughout his life, Taylor's
personal reticence and tact

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:10.890
served the cause of
interracial diplomacy.

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:15.030
He traveled often to New York
to meet with trustees or donors.

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:19.110
In 1923, he was the ranking
administrator on campus

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:21.720
when the Ku Klux
Klan paraded through.

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:24.630
The clan was trying to
intimidate the Institute

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:26.770
into accepting white
medical staffing

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:29.430
for the adjacent
Veterans Administration

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:31.890
hospital for black patients.

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:35.760
Taylor stared the clan down,
while Tuskegee's principal,

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:39.630
Robert Russa Moton
and the NAACP were

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:44.790
negotiating the issue in
Washington D.C. Tuskegee won.

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:47.430
Taylor helped investigate
the refugee camps

00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:52.170
for African-Americans displaced
by the 1927 Mississippi River

00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:56.440
flood, concluding those in which
blacks shared in the management

00:26:56.440 --> 00:27:00.140
were far better than those
in which they did not.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:03.840
His diplomatic skills joined his
educational vision and design

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:07.980
talents in 1929 when
he traveled to Liberia

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:11.110
to help found the Booker
Washington Institute.

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:14.790
Taylor analyzed Liberia's
agricultural and industrial

00:27:14.790 --> 00:27:18.970
potential, outlined a
curriculum to support it,

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:21.600
advised on governments
and staffing,

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:24.690
sketched a campus plan, and
designed a few buildings.

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:27.330
He then capped the
trip and his career

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:29.490
with a grand tour of Europe.

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:34.200
In 1932, the 64-year-old-- and
I don't know that he's 64 there,

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:36.580
but just deal with it.

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:39.930
The 64-year-old Robert R.
Taylor suffered a massive heart

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:40.850
attack.

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:43.950
He was visiting
his son in Chicago.

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:47.640
He retired shortly thereafter,
returning to Wilmington

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.660
so he would not
embarrass his successor.

00:27:50.660 --> 00:27:54.334
There, he lived a gentleman's
life with a townhouse.

00:27:54.334 --> 00:27:55.500
I don't know that he did it.

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:57.060
I think, probably,
he did not, but we

00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:59.770
need to learn about that one.

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:05.740
With a townhouse and a waterside
retreat about 20 miles away.

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:09.790
He had a car and a driver so
he could travel between them.

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:13.160
Taylor had another 10
years and used them well

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:16.590
as an admired townsman
whose word mattered.

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:19.440
He served as the first
African-American on the board

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:22.650
of a black state college, and
he worked with a powerful white

00:28:22.650 --> 00:28:25.590
man, as it happened-- and
this, I think, is amazing--

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:29.520
a leader of the
1898 massacre to try

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:33.540
for New Deal funding
to help local farmers

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:35.205
gain better status
to get a better

00:28:35.205 --> 00:28:38.760
community for local farmers,
and recreational facilities

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:40.670
for the black teenagers.

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:43.800
Taylor wanted to return to
Liberia to continue that work,

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:46.140
but his health
did not permit it.

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:50.870
He died in the Tuskegee chapel
in 1942 while on a visit.

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:54.650
He had just pronounced the
building his masterpiece.

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:58.110
Taylor left a record of
kindness and generosity,

00:28:58.110 --> 00:29:01.710
along with a becoming modesty
about his own achievements.

00:29:01.710 --> 00:29:05.430
He was a lovely man to know,
his daughter-in-law told me.

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:07.770
She never would have
known about this MIT

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:09.780
degree had not others told her.

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:11.860
When I complained
to a grand-niece

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:13.890
it was hard to
find out about him,

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:16.510
she replied and
with some asperity,

00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:20.550
I thought, that he was too busy
helping humanity to keep up

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:22.500
records about himself.

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:25.590
Taylor believed in work
for its own satisfaction,

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:29.820
and attention to detail,
financial prudence, religion

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:32.680
for the solace it can
bring one, keeping

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:35.410
one's word, and service.

00:29:35.410 --> 00:29:39.040
Finally, when I asked his son
if Taylor thought he was more

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:42.420
an educator or an
architect, Edward Taylor

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:44.160
split the difference.

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:47.400
Robert R. Taylor thought
of himself as an educator,

00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:49.380
and here the pause
was deliberate,

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:51.230
but he knew that he
was an architect.

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:52.428
Thank you.

00:29:52.428 --> 00:29:54.898
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:16.634 --> 00:30:18.660
SPEAKER 1: Thank you,
Ellen, for that talk.

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:20.390
Before I introduce
our keynote speaker,

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:22.515
I'd like to give you some
background and the reason

00:30:22.515 --> 00:30:24.000
for this conference.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.392
Two years ago, the committee was
curated under Stanford Anderson

00:30:27.392 --> 00:30:28.850
from the Department
of Architecture

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:32.910
to steady the situation with
under-represented minorities.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.582
There are many parts of that
study, and many of them,

00:30:35.582 --> 00:30:38.040
which can be discussed, perhaps,
later on in the conference

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:39.930
later tomorrow.

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:43.602
There was one element to say
the least that was shocking.

00:30:43.602 --> 00:30:45.060
We discovered that
we had graduated

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:48.020
only six African-Americans
at the M-Arch level

00:30:48.020 --> 00:30:55.380
in the past nine years, versus
2,210 non-African-Americans.

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:57.150
So that's roughly
three African-Americans

00:30:57.150 --> 00:31:00.130
for every thousand
non-African-Americans.

00:31:00.130 --> 00:31:02.850
Meaning that MIT had
done a terrible job

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:05.735
recognizing the problem,
much less providing

00:31:05.735 --> 00:31:07.779
any leadership in it.

00:31:07.779 --> 00:31:09.320
Perhaps it was
because the department

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:11.730
was so international, with
people and languages from all

00:31:11.730 --> 00:31:13.950
over the world filling
our corridors that we

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:15.360
didn't notice.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:17.790
I don't know, but what I
do know is that the failure

00:31:17.790 --> 00:31:21.700
to recognize this low graduation
rate was a mistake on our part,

00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:23.610
and there's no sense
to sugarcoat it now.

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:36.100
The graph that I show here
is roughly the graduations

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:37.130
in the last 100 years.

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:43.260
Whereas women have risen
remarkably over the last 30

00:31:43.260 --> 00:31:44.950
years, and constituted
here at MIT,

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:48.756
as in most places in the
US, 50% of the student body,

00:31:48.756 --> 00:31:50.550
the story of
African-American students

00:31:50.550 --> 00:31:54.720
is not so rosy, nor
even so promising.

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:56.910
We graduated no more
African-Americans today

00:31:56.910 --> 00:32:00.510
than we did in the late 1950s.

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:02.690
In fact, given the
bump in the late 60s--

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:04.440
you can sort of see
there's a little bit--

00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:07.110
it goes up in the
1960s and early 70s,

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:11.250
in which there are several
alumni here in the audience.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:13.680
We can say that we
are doing, perhaps,

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:20.130
even worse than we did in
the 1960s after 50 years.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:22.350
Now, I don't know if
other schools are facing

00:32:22.350 --> 00:32:25.380
similar situations,
I do suspect so,

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:28.320
but that doesn't change
the message that we have

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:31.410
for you here today, which is
that the first step to change

00:32:31.410 --> 00:32:34.170
is certainly to recognize
our own failures,

00:32:34.170 --> 00:32:36.330
and this we have to
do quite publicly.

00:32:36.330 --> 00:32:39.280
It is the first step
to rebuilding trust.

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:41.150
Part of the reason
for this conference

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:44.520
is therefore to learn how
to speak of the problem,

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:47.120
and it's not necessarily
an easy task.

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:50.130
I for one, had to learn
how to do it myself,

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:52.190
and it began when I talked
with David Sledge, who

00:32:52.190 --> 00:32:56.010
is coming in from Georgia.

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:59.280
Stuck somewhere, I think.

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:03.120
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:05.520
SPEAKER 1: There he is.

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:08.980
It was [INAUDIBLE] student,
some five years ago,

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:10.720
something like that,
and I asked him

00:33:10.720 --> 00:33:12.760
one day out of the
blue what it was

00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:15.894
like to be the only black
student in the department,

00:33:15.894 --> 00:33:18.310
and he answered that it was
the first time anyone here had

00:33:18.310 --> 00:33:21.370
asked him that question at MIT.

00:33:21.370 --> 00:33:22.900
I didn't really
understand the depth

00:33:22.900 --> 00:33:26.920
of what he had meant at first,
but it began to slowly sink in.

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:29.590
So I thank him for
that conversation,

00:33:29.590 --> 00:33:32.624
and for the conversations
we've had since.

00:33:32.624 --> 00:33:34.540
I've learned from Larry
Sasse who teaches here

00:33:34.540 --> 00:33:37.060
in the department about
issues of tokenism,

00:33:37.060 --> 00:33:40.930
and questions of mentoring,
and how, in some sense,

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:42.430
the whole issue of
African-Americans

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:45.430
is a question of discourse.

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:47.980
And I learned from conversations
with Dan Hendricks, who

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:50.330
is also sitting somewhere here.

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:51.040
Up there.

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:56.270
With conversations
about him, and it

00:33:56.270 --> 00:33:58.930
was these conversations that
led us to the conference itself

00:33:58.930 --> 00:34:00.230
when we sat around,
and we said, well,

00:34:00.230 --> 00:34:02.021
we have to do more than
just talk about it.

00:34:02.021 --> 00:34:04.030
We have to bring
in people to have

00:34:04.030 --> 00:34:07.390
an open and frank discussion
about teaching, administration,

00:34:07.390 --> 00:34:10.553
and about architecture,
about the profession.

00:34:10.553 --> 00:34:12.219
And to learn to see
the problem not just

00:34:12.219 --> 00:34:15.570
as an African-American issue,
but as a broad cultural issue

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:18.900
in the field of architecture
and architectural education,

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:21.100
for which all of
us, white and black,

00:34:21.100 --> 00:34:24.340
national and international,
should have a claim.

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:27.900
There is no magic
bullet, that is for sure.

00:34:27.900 --> 00:34:30.085
The issue is complex
and multi-faceted.

00:34:30.085 --> 00:34:32.980
But 10 years from now, we
will be in a better position

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:35.920
than we are today, that
much I guarantee you.

00:34:35.920 --> 00:34:38.199
10 years from now, we hope
that MIT will no longer

00:34:38.199 --> 00:34:41.310
be seen as an example
of the problem,

00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:43.812
but as an example
of the solution.

00:34:43.812 --> 00:34:45.770
The Chairman of the
Department of Architecture,

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:51.739
Yung Ho-Chang, who is somewhere
over there, Dean Adele Santos,

00:34:51.739 --> 00:34:54.565
Provost, and Culbert, and
others at MIT's administration

00:34:54.565 --> 00:34:56.940
have committed MIT to
understanding the problem,

00:34:56.940 --> 00:35:00.240
and to working with
us on this issue.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:03.970
So on these two notes, which
are the note of honesty

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:07.970
and of optimism, I would like to
introduce our keynote speaker,

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:09.386
Ted Landsmark.

00:35:09.386 --> 00:35:11.450
He's known nationally
and internationally

00:35:11.450 --> 00:35:12.950
for his commitment
to the questions

00:35:12.950 --> 00:35:15.520
of architectural education,
and as a spokesperson

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:16.990
of the African-American cause.

00:35:16.990 --> 00:35:19.840
He's the director of the
Boston Architectural College,

00:35:19.840 --> 00:35:21.960
located just across the
river in the Back Bay

00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:25.370
and I'm glad he didn't get swept
into the river walking over

00:35:25.370 --> 00:35:26.922
here.

00:35:26.922 --> 00:35:29.380
He's received fellowships from
the Museum of Early Southern

00:35:29.380 --> 00:35:31.990
Decorative Arts, the
National Science Foundation,

00:35:31.990 --> 00:35:35.973
he serves on an editorial board
of the magazine of Architecture

00:35:35.973 --> 00:35:36.940
Boston.

00:35:36.940 --> 00:35:39.440
Landsmark also serves as
a trustee to numerous arts

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:41.760
related foundations,
including Boston's Institute

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:43.900
of Contemporary Art,
and Museum of Fine Arts.

00:35:43.900 --> 00:35:46.858
Ted, welcome to MIT.

00:35:46.858 --> 00:35:49.323
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:04.630
LANDSMARK: Wow, this
isn't pointing this right.

00:36:04.630 --> 00:36:05.765
How do I advance this?

00:36:22.360 --> 00:36:29.160
Excuse me, while we
do some technology,

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:33.115
I've served as President of
the Association of Collegiate

00:36:33.115 --> 00:36:37.115
Schools of Architecture over
the course of the past year,

00:36:37.115 --> 00:36:39.730
and in that capacity,
I've had an opportunity

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:46.180
to get to about 70 of the
accredited architecture

00:36:46.180 --> 00:36:50.090
programs around the country,
of which there are now

00:36:50.090 --> 00:36:56.350
either 116 or 117, and
we'll talk that differential

00:36:56.350 --> 00:36:57.715
towards the end of my comments.

00:37:02.050 --> 00:37:04.900
One thing that's quite clear
is that the kinds of issues

00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:10.240
that you're trying
to address here--

00:37:10.240 --> 00:37:13.070
hopefully, this booming will go
away in terms of my microphone.

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:15.790
But the kinds of
issues that we're

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:18.010
being called upon
to address here

00:37:18.010 --> 00:37:22.210
are, obviously, not
listening to MIT.

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:26.470
I taught here in the Department
of Urban Studies and Planning

00:37:26.470 --> 00:37:30.040
a number of years ago,
back in the late 70s,

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:33.365
at a point in time when a lot of
these issues were being raised,

00:37:33.365 --> 00:37:36.130
a lot of this talked
about it at the time.

00:37:36.130 --> 00:37:37.660
A lot of us tried
to do something,

00:37:37.660 --> 00:37:39.790
but the issues
have not gone away,

00:37:39.790 --> 00:37:42.210
as I think everyone knows.

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:47.530
I'm going to talk a little bit
about race and architecture.

00:37:47.530 --> 00:37:50.890
I'm going to talk a little bit
about architectural education

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:54.340
as a broader context for
trying to understand some

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:57.970
of the issues that we need
to address if we're going

00:37:57.970 --> 00:38:02.080
to be specific about developing
some solutions for the issues

00:38:02.080 --> 00:38:04.240
of race and
architecture, and I also

00:38:04.240 --> 00:38:06.835
have to talk just a little
bit about the practice

00:38:06.835 --> 00:38:10.140
of architecture, the
profession thereof.

00:38:10.140 --> 00:38:12.380
As I think many of
you know, the VAC

00:38:12.380 --> 00:38:16.000
is a practice-based
learning institution.

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:20.590
So all of our graduates have
to work three years in firms

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:23.830
before they can graduate,
and as a consequence,

00:38:23.830 --> 00:38:27.180
I get to see a lot of what's
going on in firms these days.

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:30.190
And what's going on in
firms, to a large extent,

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:35.830
I think, replicates or echos
what has gone on in schools.

00:38:35.830 --> 00:38:40.540
I should also note
that ordinarily,

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:43.120
when I've been called upon over
the course of the past year

00:38:43.120 --> 00:38:47.670
to try to discuss these
issues, the conversation

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:51.640
is with American Institute
of Architecture groups,

00:38:51.640 --> 00:38:56.710
or various licensing
groups such as NCARB, which

00:38:56.710 --> 00:38:59.380
means that ordinarily, I'm
speaking to audiences that

00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:04.660
are 98% white, and
usually about 70% male,

00:39:04.660 --> 00:39:07.230
and ordinarily, those
groups are groups

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:10.060
that have to be persuaded
that some form of diversity

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:11.330
is a good thing.

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:14.020
That they need to think
about and address because,

00:39:14.020 --> 00:39:17.740
by and large, if there is
any American profession

00:39:17.740 --> 00:39:19.240
that has gotten
away with the kind

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:23.410
of benign neglect of
diversifying itself

00:39:23.410 --> 00:39:27.100
over the course of the last
30 years, its architecture.

00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:30.070
Law addressed these same
issues 30 years ago,

00:39:30.070 --> 00:39:33.064
medicine addressed these
same issues, business schools

00:39:33.064 --> 00:39:34.230
addressed these same issues.

00:39:34.230 --> 00:39:36.220
They developed
solutions, and when

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:37.750
you go into those
kinds of schools,

00:39:37.750 --> 00:39:39.700
you see a very
different student.

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:43.570
In fact, they make up
[INAUDIBLE] in design schools.

00:39:43.570 --> 00:39:47.770
So change is possible, but it's
only possible in the context

00:39:47.770 --> 00:39:51.230
of a broader cultural and
professional set of changes,

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:54.940
and whatever MIT
elects to do will

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:58.420
be very important, and
useful because MIT is clearly

00:39:58.420 --> 00:40:01.720
viewed as a leader
within that field

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:04.810
of architectural education.

00:40:04.810 --> 00:40:09.280
But you're also taking on
an entire profession which

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:11.650
has elected, to a
very large extent,

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:14.590
to ignore these
issues completely.

00:40:14.590 --> 00:40:18.580
There are about 100,000 licensed
architects in the United States

00:40:18.580 --> 00:40:19.240
at this moment.

00:40:19.240 --> 00:40:21.370
That's a convenient
number because it

00:40:21.370 --> 00:40:24.360
helps to get the
percentages very easily.

00:40:24.360 --> 00:40:28.370
It's probably closer right
now to about 102,000.

00:40:28.370 --> 00:40:31.060
Of that 100,000, as far as
we've been able to tell,

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:35.350
1571 are African-American,
and of that 100,000,

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:39.940
186 are African-American
women out of 100,000.

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:43.220
The upcoming issue of Architect
Magazine, as far as I know,

00:40:43.220 --> 00:40:44.275
is doing a cover story.

00:40:44.275 --> 00:40:45.060
I don't know if it's out yet.

00:40:45.060 --> 00:40:45.770
AUDIENCE: It's out.

00:40:45.770 --> 00:40:47.228
LANDSMARK: OK, I
haven't seen that,

00:40:47.228 --> 00:40:53.810
but this conference was reported
in Architectural Record,

00:40:53.810 --> 00:40:57.095
and Architect Magazine
has taken note.

00:40:57.095 --> 00:41:00.070
The magazines took note
of this 30 years ago too,

00:41:00.070 --> 00:41:02.920
and they didn't
do much about it.

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:08.530
The hope is that at this point,
these numbers will change.

00:41:08.530 --> 00:41:11.360
The Cambridge School
of Design that I'm

00:41:11.360 --> 00:41:14.470
referring to-- actually, I
have a more updated number.

00:41:14.470 --> 00:41:15.490
It's not 36.

00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:18.760
I think it's actually 48 that
that well-known Cambridge

00:41:18.760 --> 00:41:23.610
School of Design have graduated
over the last 35 or 40 years.

00:41:23.610 --> 00:41:25.007
That number shows up shortly.

00:41:25.007 --> 00:41:26.840
In the entire commonwealth
of Massachusetts,

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:31.980
there are 29 licensed
African-American architects.

00:41:31.980 --> 00:41:39.150
One J. Lee just came online, but
then we lost one through death

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:40.270
a couple of months ago.

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:42.060
So the numbers have not changed.

00:41:42.060 --> 00:41:56.050
29 out of about 3,000.

00:41:56.050 --> 00:42:01.530
This slide, I normally have to
put up for my white audiences.

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:03.750
Here, presumably the
fact that you're here

00:42:03.750 --> 00:42:07.050
in the midst of a
blizzard and northeaster

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:10.020
means that you get it.

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:12.720
This jumped twice.

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:16.290
Diversity is important because
the market that they serve

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:17.500
has become diversified.

00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:21.900
It is global, and as long as
the American Architectural

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:24.270
profession purports
to be in a position

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:29.430
to understand the cultural,
ergonomic, aesthetic values

00:42:29.430 --> 00:42:34.260
of most of the world,
and is a profession that

00:42:34.260 --> 00:42:39.000
remains with 1.5%
African-American participation

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:41.730
in that profession, we can't
really hold ourselves out

00:42:41.730 --> 00:42:45.510
to be the kind of profession
that can really serve

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:47.380
the world very effectively.

00:42:47.380 --> 00:42:49.980
The total number of architects
in the United States

00:42:49.980 --> 00:42:56.150
has increased very slightly over
the course of the last decade

00:42:56.150 --> 00:42:56.650
or so.

00:42:56.650 --> 00:42:59.420
There was a slight dip,
which was brought on

00:42:59.420 --> 00:43:02.120
in part by a recession
in the early 90s.

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:04.460
That doesn't show up here,
but the actual numbers

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:06.385
of architects in
the United States

00:43:06.385 --> 00:43:09.433
have not changed dramatically.

00:43:09.433 --> 00:43:14.690
Here's the why is this
important question.

00:43:14.690 --> 00:43:17.480
This is a profession
that tends, particularly

00:43:17.480 --> 00:43:21.320
when one focuses on the
effects of modernism

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:24.590
on architectural
education and the like,

00:43:24.590 --> 00:43:27.290
to hold out that it's
possible to plant

00:43:27.290 --> 00:43:30.270
the same kind of building
anywhere in the world.

00:43:30.270 --> 00:43:31.940
But in fact, there
are many of us

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:34.160
who believe that that
does a disservice

00:43:34.160 --> 00:43:38.250
to the clients in those
varying parts of the world.

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:41.870
And what we've found
is that certainly

00:43:41.870 --> 00:43:43.700
from a marketing point
of view, and this

00:43:43.700 --> 00:43:47.500
is what I always have to
explain to affirm principles,

00:43:47.500 --> 00:43:49.130
being a more diverse
firm opens more

00:43:49.130 --> 00:43:51.710
opportunities for that firm.

00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:55.280
It creates a better kind
of and richer dialogue

00:43:55.280 --> 00:43:59.240
within the firm, as more
people of diverse backgrounds

00:43:59.240 --> 00:44:00.980
bring different kinds
of perspectives.

00:44:00.980 --> 00:44:03.290
I remember when I was
at Yale, for example,

00:44:03.290 --> 00:44:07.010
and we were asked to design
a public housing development,

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:11.240
some of my classmates actually
planted a baby grand piano

00:44:11.240 --> 00:44:14.660
in the drawings
that they did, and I

00:44:14.660 --> 00:44:16.160
as a kid who grew
up in the projects

00:44:16.160 --> 00:44:19.070
knew that that was ridiculous,
but as of the time that I

00:44:19.070 --> 00:44:20.660
was in school,
those students had

00:44:20.660 --> 00:44:24.440
no idea of the
inappropriateness of that.

00:44:24.440 --> 00:44:27.194
Plus diversity is the
right thing for us to do.

00:44:27.194 --> 00:44:28.610
The other thing
that's interesting

00:44:28.610 --> 00:44:32.690
is that there is more of a need
for architects in the world

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:35.780
today than at any
time in our history,

00:44:35.780 --> 00:44:40.670
but the profession itself
is not growing as such.

00:44:40.670 --> 00:44:45.030
So to a large extent, whether
it's because of the objections

00:44:45.030 --> 00:44:49.160
that many practitioners raised,
and engineers, and developers,

00:44:49.160 --> 00:44:55.460
and builders are taking away
all of the work of architects,

00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:57.140
that's one issue.

00:44:57.140 --> 00:45:00.290
But the larger issue is
that the profession itself

00:45:00.290 --> 00:45:02.840
has not embraced what
its responsibility is

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:05.276
to really provide service
to all of the people

00:45:05.276 --> 00:45:06.650
in the world who
need architects.

00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:09.980
So in a funny, but
not so funny way,

00:45:09.980 --> 00:45:12.320
this profession is the
only major profession

00:45:12.320 --> 00:45:15.170
that I've found in the United
States that in proportion

00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:17.850
to the world need, is
actually shrinking,

00:45:17.850 --> 00:45:19.970
and it's becoming a
niche profession that

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:22.730
celebrates a certain
kind of architecture,

00:45:22.730 --> 00:45:26.540
but not, in fact, the
kind of architecture

00:45:26.540 --> 00:45:28.880
that needs to be done.

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:34.340
When I've done this presentation
to student groups, which again,

00:45:34.340 --> 00:45:37.122
are primarily white, and
young, and full of enthusiasm,

00:45:37.122 --> 00:45:39.330
and really terrific kids,
and all that sort of stuff,

00:45:39.330 --> 00:45:41.270
the first thing I have
to point out to them

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:44.060
is that in 25 years,
their clients will not

00:45:44.060 --> 00:45:47.120
look like what they
look like, and that they

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:50.240
need to get used to that
idea, and figure out

00:45:50.240 --> 00:45:51.340
how to adjust to that.

00:45:56.210 --> 00:45:57.740
It's an interesting
slide in that

00:45:57.740 --> 00:46:01.070
it talks about the percentages.

00:46:01.070 --> 00:46:05.450
When we look at
American professions,

00:46:05.450 --> 00:46:07.730
where do architects
stand in relation

00:46:07.730 --> 00:46:11.291
to other kinds of professions?

00:46:11.291 --> 00:46:15.530
The 129,000 figure is
a somewhat soft figure.

00:46:15.530 --> 00:46:18.720
In fact, a census data--

00:46:18.720 --> 00:46:22.250
the most recent
census data, argue

00:46:22.250 --> 00:46:25.550
that there are almost 200,000
people in the United States

00:46:25.550 --> 00:46:28.460
who declare themselves
to be architects.

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:30.830
Only half of those are licensed.

00:46:30.830 --> 00:46:34.190
So somewhere between
100,000 to 200,000,

00:46:34.190 --> 00:46:38.690
there is a number of people who
are practicing this profession.

00:46:38.690 --> 00:46:40.790
That number is not
important in of in itself.

00:46:40.790 --> 00:46:45.020
What's important is that in
relation to other professions,

00:46:45.020 --> 00:46:48.530
basically there are 10
accountants for every architect

00:46:48.530 --> 00:46:50.570
in the United States.

00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:53.540
There are five doctors
for every architect.

00:46:53.540 --> 00:46:56.330
There are more than 10
engineers for every architect.

00:46:56.330 --> 00:47:01.490
So we start with a small number
of individuals who are actually

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:04.740
practicing this profession.

00:47:04.740 --> 00:47:07.770
And there's always
been discussion

00:47:07.770 --> 00:47:10.320
about why it is that
African-Americans and other

00:47:10.320 --> 00:47:13.124
people of color don't
go into this field,

00:47:13.124 --> 00:47:14.540
and often, it's
not-- the argument

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:18.120
is put forward that the
smart black guy isn't

00:47:18.120 --> 00:47:19.900
going to become an
architect because he

00:47:19.900 --> 00:47:21.030
can't make as much money.

00:47:21.030 --> 00:47:23.640
He's going to become
a lawyer instead,

00:47:23.640 --> 00:47:26.632
like I became a lawyer rather
than becoming an architect.

00:47:26.632 --> 00:47:28.090
But the reality of
the circumstance

00:47:28.090 --> 00:47:33.890
is that in comparison
with other professions,

00:47:33.890 --> 00:47:36.937
architects earn a living wage.

00:47:36.937 --> 00:47:39.020
And what we've discovered
from some of the polling

00:47:39.020 --> 00:47:41.540
that we did, some of the studies
that the American Institute

00:47:41.540 --> 00:47:45.920
of Architects undertook two
years ago, what we discovered

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:48.020
is that, in fact, the
people going to architect

00:47:48.020 --> 00:47:49.910
don't go into architecture
for the money.

00:47:49.910 --> 00:47:51.580
None of us do that.

00:47:51.580 --> 00:47:55.520
We go into it because
we like to make stuff.

00:47:55.520 --> 00:47:56.680
We're builders.

00:47:56.680 --> 00:47:57.790
We're makers of things.

00:47:57.790 --> 00:48:01.900
We're designers of things, and
that the economic incentives

00:48:01.900 --> 00:48:05.200
in and of themselves
are not sufficient

00:48:05.200 --> 00:48:08.620
disincentives for people
to go into this field.

00:48:08.620 --> 00:48:12.460
Indeed, when one looks at
the AIAs old figures in terms

00:48:12.460 --> 00:48:16.769
of what it is that architects
earn in the United States,

00:48:16.769 --> 00:48:18.935
one would have to argue
that, in fact, most of these

00:48:18.935 --> 00:48:22.510
are living wages, and I know
that our BAC graduates, when

00:48:22.510 --> 00:48:25.450
they come out of school, if
they know a little bit of Revit,

00:48:25.450 --> 00:48:29.320
are starting in the
$50,000 to $55,000

00:48:29.320 --> 00:48:32.600
range, which is not a
bad starting salary.

00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:34.600
It takes you a long
time to get there,

00:48:34.600 --> 00:48:36.560
but it's hardly a poverty wage.

00:48:36.560 --> 00:48:39.460
And that, in fact,
principals of major firms

00:48:39.460 --> 00:48:43.810
in this country, owners,
are earning six figures,

00:48:43.810 --> 00:48:46.160
and some good six figures.

00:48:46.160 --> 00:48:49.709
So the argument that's often
put forward that people of color

00:48:49.709 --> 00:48:51.250
don't go into this
field because they

00:48:51.250 --> 00:48:55.250
can't earn enough money in it
is pretty much a bogus argument.

00:48:55.250 --> 00:48:57.100
And then when people
say that to me,

00:48:57.100 --> 00:49:00.950
I also flip the argument
around and ask, well,

00:49:00.950 --> 00:49:03.040
if people of color
are too smart to go

00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:05.210
into this low paying field,
what's wrong with all

00:49:05.210 --> 00:49:07.290
the white men who do?

00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:10.600
Are they all really coming
from white shoe backgrounds?

00:49:10.600 --> 00:49:13.330
The answer is no, they're not.

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:18.780
So the economic side of
all of this is bogus.

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:20.490
Because there's
so much text here,

00:49:20.490 --> 00:49:24.860
I've added some slides
of some of the architects

00:49:24.860 --> 00:49:25.910
we ought to think about.

00:49:25.910 --> 00:49:29.500
Those people who have, in fact,
constituted the foundation

00:49:29.500 --> 00:49:31.083
of black architecture
in this country.

00:49:34.330 --> 00:49:37.600
So we know that there
are 100,000 architects,

00:49:37.600 --> 00:49:41.260
and we're going to get into some
more detail here around that.

00:49:41.260 --> 00:49:44.240
Hopefully it will be discussed
in further detail tomorrow.

00:49:47.080 --> 00:49:51.490
There are about 33,000 folks
in our architecture schools,

00:49:51.490 --> 00:49:53.440
and we'll get to that
number in a moment.

00:49:53.440 --> 00:49:58.030
But of the 200,000 people
who declared themselves

00:49:58.030 --> 00:50:00.190
to be architects,
roughly 20% are

00:50:00.190 --> 00:50:05.050
women, a little bit less than 3%
are African-American, nearly 6%

00:50:05.050 --> 00:50:06.540
are either Hispanic or Latino.

00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:09.800
It's hard to count Hispanics
and Latinos because

00:50:09.800 --> 00:50:12.400
of self-declaration issues.

00:50:12.400 --> 00:50:18.970
Roughly 6% are Asian, 3% say
that they are American Indian.

00:50:18.970 --> 00:50:23.260
So technically,
as among those who

00:50:23.260 --> 00:50:26.930
declare themselves to
be architects, about 15%

00:50:26.930 --> 00:50:30.670
are so-called people of color.

00:50:30.670 --> 00:50:33.415
In NCARB, the licensing
body at this point,

00:50:33.415 --> 00:50:37.510
reports about 101,000
licensed architects.

00:50:37.510 --> 00:50:40.050
Part of the difficulty that
we have in this profession

00:50:40.050 --> 00:50:43.270
is that there's no central
database from which one

00:50:43.270 --> 00:50:47.320
can draw information that tells
you what the real number is

00:50:47.320 --> 00:50:51.610
because, in fact, NCARB,
the national licensing body,

00:50:51.610 --> 00:50:55.150
draws its information by calling
around to all of the state

00:50:55.150 --> 00:50:59.170
licensing bodies, and since
many architects are registered

00:50:59.170 --> 00:51:02.350
in more than one
state, there isn't

00:51:02.350 --> 00:51:04.360
a way at this moment
of getting one

00:51:04.360 --> 00:51:07.990
definitive number on the total
number of licensed architects.

00:51:07.990 --> 00:51:10.210
But of the 80,000 members
of the American Institute

00:51:10.210 --> 00:51:12.970
of Architects,
interestingly, only 12%

00:51:12.970 --> 00:51:18.730
are women, despite the fact that
for at least 10, 12, 15 years,

00:51:18.730 --> 00:51:21.400
half of the students in all
of our architecture programs

00:51:21.400 --> 00:51:23.510
in the country have been women.

00:51:23.510 --> 00:51:27.350
Only 12% of the members
of the American Institute

00:51:27.350 --> 00:51:31.695
of Architects are women,
2% are Hispanic or Latino,

00:51:31.695 --> 00:51:35.780
1.5% are black, 3% are Asian.

00:51:35.780 --> 00:51:38.880
We also did some surveys
a couple of years ago,

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:41.780
now, of people who
are practicing,

00:51:41.780 --> 00:51:45.680
but not members of the American
Institute of Architects.

00:51:45.680 --> 00:51:48.554
And interestingly, of those
numbers, about 10% are women.

00:51:48.554 --> 00:51:50.345
Well, you can see the
numbers for yourself.

00:51:58.920 --> 00:52:03.090
Architecture school graduates
are interesting as well.

00:52:03.090 --> 00:52:07.430
The total number of architecture
school students in the country

00:52:07.430 --> 00:52:11.310
has varied minimally
in the last 20 years.

00:52:11.310 --> 00:52:14.820
It's varied from
about a low of 31,000

00:52:14.820 --> 00:52:18.300
to a high of about
33,000, 34,000.

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:22.350
The actual number of
graduates in any given year

00:52:22.350 --> 00:52:26.661
is about 3,000 from all of
our programs across the United

00:52:26.661 --> 00:52:27.160
States.

00:52:31.810 --> 00:52:38.130
And in comparison with who is
in higher education overall,

00:52:38.130 --> 00:52:42.910
and I have several layers
of data here, more than half

00:52:42.910 --> 00:52:46.660
of all of the students enrolled
in higher ed in the United

00:52:46.660 --> 00:52:48.570
States are now women.

00:52:48.570 --> 00:52:52.180
A little bit less than 10%
are African-American, about 6%

00:52:52.180 --> 00:52:54.315
Latino, about 6% Asian.

00:52:58.090 --> 00:53:01.750
In Masters programs,
interestingly, nearly 60%

00:53:01.750 --> 00:53:06.400
are women, about 7% to
8% are African-American,

00:53:06.400 --> 00:53:10.930
a little over 4% are
Hispanic, almost 5% are Asian.

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:21.210
And there's among those
getting master's degrees

00:53:21.210 --> 00:53:25.480
in this country, only about
2% of all master's degrees

00:53:25.480 --> 00:53:29.370
that are awarded in the country
are awarded in architecture.

00:53:29.370 --> 00:53:33.431
The vast majority are in
business, engineering, law,

00:53:33.431 --> 00:53:33.930
health care.

00:53:33.930 --> 00:53:36.510
This does not count
education fields,

00:53:36.510 --> 00:53:40.040
where there are huge
numbers of individuals,

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:42.000
and EEDs, and PhDs.

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:45.480
But as among traditionally
defined professional degrees,

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:49.140
architecture amounts to only
2% of all of the degrees

00:53:49.140 --> 00:53:51.270
that are awarded
in any given year.

00:53:55.670 --> 00:54:01.120
So of our 33,000, almost
34,000 architecture students,

00:54:01.120 --> 00:54:07.750
a little bit less than a third
would be considered minority,

00:54:07.750 --> 00:54:11.779
and as you break it down in
terms of levels of programs--

00:54:11.779 --> 00:54:14.070
and these numbers start to
get particularly interesting

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:14.970
for us.

00:54:14.970 --> 00:54:18.840
In pre-professional
degrees, there

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:21.390
were only 116
African-American graduates

00:54:21.390 --> 00:54:24.150
of pre-professional
degrees in 2003,

00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:27.600
which was the most recent
year we had data on this.

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:34.350
In our B-Arch programs,
there were only 116 graduates

00:54:34.350 --> 00:54:39.710
from B-Arch programs
nationally in 2003.

00:54:39.710 --> 00:54:44.450
In our master's programs,
there were only 40 graduates

00:54:44.450 --> 00:54:49.040
of M-Arch programs in 2003
who were African-American.

00:54:52.640 --> 00:54:54.320
And now we get to the
really hard stuff,

00:54:54.320 --> 00:54:57.570
which is to say where are those
African-Americans coming from?

00:54:57.570 --> 00:55:00.530
And it turns out
that 37%, according

00:55:00.530 --> 00:55:06.940
to data that's been developed
by Dennis Mann and Brad Grant

00:55:06.940 --> 00:55:09.110
through the University
of Cincinnati,

00:55:09.110 --> 00:55:11.840
37% of all
African-American architects

00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:15.270
hold at least one degree from
an historically black college

00:55:15.270 --> 00:55:16.220
or university.

00:55:16.220 --> 00:55:19.460
That means that of
the 117 programs

00:55:19.460 --> 00:55:21.820
that are now accredited
in the country,

00:55:21.820 --> 00:55:26.210
the seven HBCUs are
turning out 37%.

00:55:26.210 --> 00:55:29.600
Or roughly 5% of all of
the programs in the country

00:55:29.600 --> 00:55:33.860
are turning out nearly 40% of
the African-American graduates.

00:55:33.860 --> 00:55:36.650
So what are those schools?

00:55:36.650 --> 00:55:38.090
And again, this is from--

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:42.020
this was actually updated
just about six months ago.

00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:45.020
Howard has turned out the
largest number, followed

00:55:45.020 --> 00:55:50.270
by Hampton, Tuskegee, Southern
down in Baton Rouge, Florida

00:55:50.270 --> 00:55:55.140
A&amp;M, which is not an accredited
program, Prairie View in Texas,

00:55:55.140 --> 00:55:58.580
Tennessee State, which is
not an accredited program,

00:55:58.580 --> 00:56:00.092
and Oregon State.

00:56:00.092 --> 00:56:02.300
It's also interesting to
see when these programs were

00:56:02.300 --> 00:56:05.602
accredited in relation to how
many graduates have come out

00:56:05.602 --> 00:56:06.328
of them.

00:56:09.510 --> 00:56:11.780
And then what about
the rest of us?

00:56:11.780 --> 00:56:15.465
What about the so-called
majority schools?

00:56:15.465 --> 00:56:17.280
And here,
increasingly, I've only

00:56:17.280 --> 00:56:22.020
listed the schools
that can say that they

00:56:22.020 --> 00:56:26.370
have 20 or more licensed
African-American graduates.

00:56:26.370 --> 00:56:31.740
Columbia's at the top, CCNY,
Urbana, Harvard, that's

00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:34.980
the 48 number that
we've come upon,

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:38.460
Berkeley, Cornell, Michigan,
MIT reports a total

00:56:38.460 --> 00:56:46.250
of 30, Tech, Detroit Mercy,
NYIT, my Alma mater, Yale,

00:56:46.250 --> 00:56:48.080
and IIT.

00:56:48.080 --> 00:56:49.790
There are some
interesting patterns here

00:56:49.790 --> 00:56:56.990
in terms of urban-rural,
east coast, central, Chicago,

00:56:56.990 --> 00:56:58.810
Berkeley.

00:56:58.810 --> 00:57:01.388
None of us have done
well is the bottom line.

00:57:04.810 --> 00:57:08.950
Back in 1968 with the young
then head of the National

00:57:08.950 --> 00:57:12.820
Urban League made a speech
to the American Institute

00:57:12.820 --> 00:57:15.130
of Architects
national convention.

00:57:15.130 --> 00:57:20.200
He chastised the profession
because in 1968, only 1%

00:57:20.200 --> 00:57:22.450
of all the licensed architects
in the United States

00:57:22.450 --> 00:57:23.790
were African-American.

00:57:23.790 --> 00:57:28.160
Today, we've gotten
it up to 1.5%.

00:57:28.160 --> 00:57:31.400
We are almost certainly the
worst profession in the United

00:57:31.400 --> 00:57:34.040
States for having made
any kind of change

00:57:34.040 --> 00:57:35.240
over the last 40 years.

00:57:38.560 --> 00:57:42.520
And I hate making reference
to this particular image,

00:57:42.520 --> 00:57:43.610
but it's true.

00:57:43.610 --> 00:57:50.490
If we were to triple the
number of African-Americans who

00:57:50.490 --> 00:57:53.850
graduated from our programs
over the next decade,

00:57:53.850 --> 00:57:55.590
we'd still only
be up around 10%.

00:57:58.860 --> 00:58:01.170
And the fact of the matter
is most of our programs

00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:02.970
aren't anywhere
close to tripling

00:58:02.970 --> 00:58:05.340
the numbers of our graduates.

00:58:05.340 --> 00:58:08.760
So the fact of the matter is
that there is every probability

00:58:08.760 --> 00:58:11.460
that unless we
take radical steps,

00:58:11.460 --> 00:58:15.870
someone else, 15 years from now,
will stand on this platform,

00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:18.844
and will be able to use
exactly the same slides

00:58:18.844 --> 00:58:19.635
with minor updates.

00:58:27.410 --> 00:58:29.000
One of the issues
that always comes up

00:58:29.000 --> 00:58:34.730
is around what happens
after folks graduate.

00:58:34.730 --> 00:58:37.360
How long does it take to get
through the architectural

00:58:37.360 --> 00:58:40.490
registration exam?

00:58:40.490 --> 00:58:43.770
And to what extent
is there an impact--

00:58:43.770 --> 00:58:47.480
a differential impact-- on
people of color in terms

00:58:47.480 --> 00:58:52.475
of getting to licensure
once they've graduate?

00:58:52.475 --> 00:58:53.850
And the reality
is that we didn't

00:58:53.850 --> 00:58:58.910
find a substantial difference,
although what we did find

00:58:58.910 --> 00:59:02.090
is that in significant numbers,
which presumably will be talked

00:59:02.090 --> 00:59:05.070
about tomorrow,
there is a higher

00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:08.940
probability that a
graduate of an HBCU

00:59:08.940 --> 00:59:13.350
will opt not to go through
the abuse of going to work

00:59:13.350 --> 00:59:16.470
for a firm in the areas
where most of the HBCUs

00:59:16.470 --> 00:59:18.870
are located for a
three-year internship

00:59:18.870 --> 00:59:22.980
to then take a licensing
exam, when they can go

00:59:22.980 --> 00:59:27.730
to work for HUD, or
the Defense Department,

00:59:27.730 --> 00:59:30.700
or the Corps of Engineers.

00:59:30.700 --> 00:59:34.930
Doing architectural work at
a higher starting salary,

00:59:34.930 --> 00:59:37.000
never have to get
a license, have

00:59:37.000 --> 00:59:40.000
a lifetime of job
security, and never go

00:59:40.000 --> 00:59:42.130
into traditional
private practice.

00:59:42.130 --> 00:59:47.210
And indeed, that appears to
account to a very large extent

00:59:47.210 --> 00:59:52.060
for what happens to graduates of
African-American HBCU programs.

00:59:52.060 --> 00:59:57.430
That is they choose the path
that is the most logical path,

00:59:57.430 --> 00:59:59.305
and they opt not to go
into private practice.

01:00:02.710 --> 01:00:04.210
And the whole
question of internship

01:00:04.210 --> 01:00:06.610
raises one other
very difficult issue

01:00:06.610 --> 01:00:11.290
within the context of the
profession as we move forward.

01:00:11.290 --> 01:00:15.340
I don't know if any of
you were participants

01:00:15.340 --> 01:00:18.580
in the American Institute
of Architects Students

01:00:18.580 --> 01:00:21.580
Annual Convention, which
occurred this year here

01:00:21.580 --> 01:00:24.110
in Boston over at the Sheraton.

01:00:24.110 --> 01:00:28.120
About 1100 students came in,
and the BAC, and Wentworth

01:00:28.120 --> 01:00:29.710
co-hosted those students.

01:00:29.710 --> 01:00:31.660
And one of the
presentations there

01:00:31.660 --> 01:00:35.620
was from a firm in India,
an outsourcing firm.

01:00:35.620 --> 01:00:38.890
The kind of firm that our
architecture firms don't

01:00:38.890 --> 01:00:41.620
like to talk about a lot
because of all the liability

01:00:41.620 --> 01:00:45.940
issues that are inherent in
sending your work to India

01:00:45.940 --> 01:00:48.610
overnight to have your
production drawings done.

01:00:48.610 --> 01:00:51.520
And until very recently,
the whole business

01:00:51.520 --> 01:00:53.270
of outsourcing
within the profession

01:00:53.270 --> 01:00:56.440
was simply something that
no one talked about at all.

01:00:56.440 --> 01:00:59.140
But all those friends are
now out of the closet,

01:00:59.140 --> 01:01:01.960
and we managed to
get a presentation

01:01:01.960 --> 01:01:06.580
from one of those firms at
the AIA meeting in Boston.

01:01:06.580 --> 01:01:09.520
And the firm
principal said, look,

01:01:09.520 --> 01:01:15.310
I have the ability to employ
1,000 people in my firm

01:01:15.310 --> 01:01:20.910
in India, or in similar firms
in Pakistan, or China, or Egypt.

01:01:20.910 --> 01:01:24.210
You do your work
here during the day,

01:01:24.210 --> 01:01:29.030
and send your work to me
electronically overnight.

01:01:29.030 --> 01:01:31.910
My 1,000 people will
turn around your work

01:01:31.910 --> 01:01:35.870
more efficiently than can
an intern in your office

01:01:35.870 --> 01:01:38.240
because they're
well-versed in Revit.

01:01:38.240 --> 01:01:40.490
They know all of
the latest software,

01:01:40.490 --> 01:01:42.620
and they're doing this
kind of work for firms

01:01:42.620 --> 01:01:44.330
all over the world.

01:01:44.330 --> 01:01:48.940
We can get the word back to
you the next morning at 1/5

01:01:48.940 --> 01:01:51.700
the cost.

01:01:51.700 --> 01:01:54.590
And so students who
are at this conference,

01:01:54.590 --> 01:01:57.170
at the end of the presentation,
went up to some of the people

01:01:57.170 --> 01:01:59.760
from NCARB and said
well, wait a minute,

01:01:59.760 --> 01:02:04.760
if the work that we as interns
used to be able to do in order

01:02:04.760 --> 01:02:08.300
to develop the skill sets
necessary to take an exam

01:02:08.300 --> 01:02:13.160
can now be done in India at
1/5 the cost, what kind of work

01:02:13.160 --> 01:02:17.610
are we as interns going to
be doing if you hire us?

01:02:17.610 --> 01:02:19.770
And none of the folks
from NCARB had an answer

01:02:19.770 --> 01:02:22.260
to that because it is
becoming increasingly

01:02:22.260 --> 01:02:25.590
clear that the kind of work
that architecture school

01:02:25.590 --> 01:02:28.200
graduates have
been doing in firms

01:02:28.200 --> 01:02:32.060
is now being outsourced
for much less money

01:02:32.060 --> 01:02:35.070
and more proficiently.

01:02:35.070 --> 01:02:39.030
We asked a series of questions
through the AIA as to what

01:02:39.030 --> 01:02:42.180
were the key reasons for
people not practicing,

01:02:42.180 --> 01:02:45.270
and we did some comparative
analysis of these.

01:02:45.270 --> 01:02:47.446
I can't go into all
the detail here.

01:02:47.446 --> 01:02:49.320
For those of you who
want to see that detail,

01:02:49.320 --> 01:02:53.421
we've posted it on
the BAC website.

01:02:53.421 --> 01:02:55.545
It was commissioned work
that was done for the AIA,

01:02:55.545 --> 01:03:00.320
and then the AIA didn't
want to release the study.

01:03:00.320 --> 01:03:03.050
But since I chaired the
diversity committee,

01:03:03.050 --> 01:03:04.910
and worked with the
firm that did the study,

01:03:04.910 --> 01:03:08.240
I thought I had some
proprietary right

01:03:08.240 --> 01:03:11.070
to hang onto the
results of the study.

01:03:11.070 --> 01:03:13.550
So for any of you
who really want

01:03:13.550 --> 01:03:17.960
to see the full 120-page study,
you can go to our website,

01:03:17.960 --> 01:03:20.370
and it's quietly posted there.

01:03:20.370 --> 01:03:23.990
But what it does
indicate is that many

01:03:23.990 --> 01:03:26.120
of the reasons for
folks not practicing

01:03:26.120 --> 01:03:30.710
in terms of differentials--
basically, race and gender,

01:03:30.710 --> 01:03:35.470
have nothing to do with
inability to do the work,

01:03:35.470 --> 01:03:39.290
unwillingness to do the work,
or even the usual bugaboo that's

01:03:39.290 --> 01:03:41.850
mentioned for women, families.

01:03:41.850 --> 01:03:45.380
That in fact, most of the
people who graduated from school

01:03:45.380 --> 01:03:48.390
have every intention of
practicing in some way, shape,

01:03:48.390 --> 01:03:52.160
or form, and that the
usual stuff of gee,

01:03:52.160 --> 01:03:53.900
I can't find enough
people because they

01:03:53.900 --> 01:03:58.630
don't want to be this
kind of work is a life.

01:03:58.630 --> 01:04:00.560
What are the normal
institutional impediments

01:04:00.560 --> 01:04:05.260
that we have come upon through
our studies in terms of entry

01:04:05.260 --> 01:04:07.762
into the profession?

01:04:07.762 --> 01:04:08.970
I might as well use the word.

01:04:08.970 --> 01:04:10.930
Someone else will at
some point, if I don't.

01:04:10.930 --> 01:04:12.220
Racism is out there.

01:04:12.220 --> 01:04:14.830
It's alive and well
in the profession.

01:04:14.830 --> 01:04:17.680
There's a lot of great
talk about yes, we're open,

01:04:17.680 --> 01:04:19.240
and yes, we want
to hire everyone,

01:04:19.240 --> 01:04:23.800
but racism is evident
in many of the firms.

01:04:23.800 --> 01:04:30.340
The patronage and class system
for obtaining private work

01:04:30.340 --> 01:04:34.210
drives a lot of what
determines who goes into work,

01:04:34.210 --> 01:04:38.020
and who can survive
in this field

01:04:38.020 --> 01:04:40.060
because we all know
the clients are--

01:04:40.060 --> 01:04:42.040
private clients,
in particular, are

01:04:42.040 --> 01:04:44.800
individuals who one meets
through social networks,

01:04:44.800 --> 01:04:47.550
and the like, and if you're not
a part of that social network,

01:04:47.550 --> 01:04:49.020
you tend not to see them.

01:04:49.020 --> 01:04:52.720
I go to many, many, many
events in the greater Boston

01:04:52.720 --> 01:04:55.180
area put on by my
friends and colleagues

01:04:55.180 --> 01:04:57.720
in the architectural
profession, and invariably, I

01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:01.610
am the only person of
color there, even today.

01:05:01.610 --> 01:05:05.590
Lack of role models,
lack of access to capital

01:05:05.590 --> 01:05:09.440
markets in order to get the
work done, and interestingly,

01:05:09.440 --> 01:05:11.520
and this came out
of a study that

01:05:11.520 --> 01:05:15.720
was done as much in Australia,
where some polling was done

01:05:15.720 --> 01:05:18.900
a number of years ago around
racial and gender differences

01:05:18.900 --> 01:05:22.080
in terms of coming
into the profession,

01:05:22.080 --> 01:05:27.960
it turns out that significant
proportions of women and people

01:05:27.960 --> 01:05:30.600
of color who decide
to go into this field

01:05:30.600 --> 01:05:32.280
have an orientation
towards doing

01:05:32.280 --> 01:05:35.410
work that would be considered
community based work.

01:05:35.410 --> 01:05:38.190
It's not the kind of work
that's celebrated, by and large,

01:05:38.190 --> 01:05:41.010
in our major publications,
or even in our schools,

01:05:41.010 --> 01:05:43.890
or in our awards patterns.

01:05:43.890 --> 01:05:47.940
We celebrate the high profile
individual corporate work,

01:05:47.940 --> 01:05:49.680
or the individual home.

01:05:49.680 --> 01:05:51.450
We tend not to
celebrate the work

01:05:51.450 --> 01:05:56.690
of the great community based
architect designs of YMCA.

01:05:56.690 --> 01:05:58.790
And as a result,
what tends to happen

01:05:58.790 --> 01:06:02.020
within the context of studio
culture and other culture

01:06:02.020 --> 01:06:05.300
is that we celebrate exactly
the kind of work, which is not

01:06:05.300 --> 01:06:07.790
the kind of work that most
women and people of color

01:06:07.790 --> 01:06:10.370
come into this field to do.

01:06:10.370 --> 01:06:12.950
And so there's not
much of an incentive as

01:06:12.950 --> 01:06:14.960
among students who
are studying this

01:06:14.960 --> 01:06:18.650
to feel as though the kind of
work that we might want to do

01:06:18.650 --> 01:06:22.040
is the kind of work that's
likely to be celebrated.

01:06:22.040 --> 01:06:25.350
Let me also note, again,
in the context of the wider

01:06:25.350 --> 01:06:29.986
profession, what the key
issues are that deans--

01:06:29.986 --> 01:06:31.700
and these are American deans--

01:06:31.700 --> 01:06:35.277
view as the key issues
that are coming up

01:06:35.277 --> 01:06:36.860
in terms of both
education, and what's

01:06:36.860 --> 01:06:39.270
happening in practice
at this moment,

01:06:39.270 --> 01:06:42.730
and these are
interesting issues.

01:06:42.730 --> 01:06:45.330
Interdisciplinary
design, effective design,

01:06:45.330 --> 01:06:46.780
and communication.

01:06:46.780 --> 01:06:51.270
Finally, there's a recognition
that many, many architects

01:06:51.270 --> 01:06:54.000
are wonderful
designers, but totally

01:06:54.000 --> 01:06:57.210
unable to express
themselves in any form other

01:06:57.210 --> 01:06:59.250
than graphically.

01:06:59.250 --> 01:07:03.240
They can't write, they can't
speak, they can't present,

01:07:03.240 --> 01:07:05.370
and architecture
firms are increasingly

01:07:05.370 --> 01:07:07.830
saying we'd like to
have some people here

01:07:07.830 --> 01:07:11.100
who know how to present their
work as well as producing it.

01:07:11.100 --> 01:07:15.420
Sustainability, of course,
technological growth,

01:07:15.420 --> 01:07:18.570
human health and safety,
and issues around licensure.

01:07:18.570 --> 01:07:22.560
And let me just say that the
licensure issue was viewed

01:07:22.560 --> 01:07:26.910
as somewhat abstract
for me until last week

01:07:26.910 --> 01:07:31.430
when I found out that the
Canadian equivalent of NCARB,

01:07:31.430 --> 01:07:35.660
our American licensing
group, has just collapsed.

01:07:35.660 --> 01:07:41.100
The four major provinces
that represent about 3/4

01:07:41.100 --> 01:07:43.110
of all of the
architects in Canada

01:07:43.110 --> 01:07:45.060
have decided to
withdraw their support

01:07:45.060 --> 01:07:47.634
from the Canadian
equivalent of NCARB,

01:07:47.634 --> 01:07:49.800
and in doing that, they've
decided that they are now

01:07:49.800 --> 01:07:53.260
going to write their
own provisional exams.

01:07:53.260 --> 01:07:54.880
And I've shared that
information when

01:07:54.880 --> 01:07:59.320
I got it with the NCARB folks
just two or three days ago

01:07:59.320 --> 01:08:01.750
in Philadelphia, and
they haven't heard

01:08:01.750 --> 01:08:04.930
it yet, but clearly, that's
going to have some ripples

01:08:04.930 --> 01:08:08.800
and repercussions for things
like international reciprocity

01:08:08.800 --> 01:08:12.970
of licensure, and the ability
to work across national borders

01:08:12.970 --> 01:08:16.300
generally, and the fact
that certain American states

01:08:16.300 --> 01:08:19.680
like California and Texas have
been moving in that direction

01:08:19.680 --> 01:08:20.200
anyway.

01:08:20.200 --> 01:08:24.270
So the question is what are
we educating our students

01:08:24.270 --> 01:08:25.800
for if we're
thinking that they're

01:08:25.800 --> 01:08:28.899
going to become
licensed at some point,

01:08:28.899 --> 01:08:30.720
if the very structure
of licensure

01:08:30.720 --> 01:08:32.550
seems to be collapsing?

01:08:32.550 --> 01:08:36.450
And that's something that
remains to be answered.

01:08:36.450 --> 01:08:39.240
BIM, that is Building
Information Modeling

01:08:39.240 --> 01:08:41.700
for those of you who
aren't in the middle of it,

01:08:41.700 --> 01:08:44.640
and performance based software
integration, the whole set

01:08:44.640 --> 01:08:48.630
of issues that comes up around
embedded intelligence in design

01:08:48.630 --> 01:08:50.520
software.

01:08:50.520 --> 01:08:53.790
Suburban and urban growth,
increasing globalization,

01:08:53.790 --> 01:08:58.529
design build processes,
project delivery.

01:08:58.529 --> 01:09:01.600
Talent shortages we'll
come to in just a moment,

01:09:01.600 --> 01:09:04.590
but I have to say that having
visited nearly 70 programs

01:09:04.590 --> 01:09:07.890
in the United States,
most of our schools

01:09:07.890 --> 01:09:11.060
are not talking
about BIM at all.

01:09:11.060 --> 01:09:13.080
We do talk about growth issues.

01:09:13.080 --> 01:09:16.344
We do talk about globalization,
but one is hard pressed

01:09:16.344 --> 01:09:18.260
to go to an architecture
school that's talking

01:09:18.260 --> 01:09:20.334
about design build processes.

01:09:20.334 --> 01:09:23.238
They're out there,
but few in number.

01:09:30.452 --> 01:09:32.160
And I've raised one
other issue, and that

01:09:32.160 --> 01:09:35.080
is to say that as
of next fall, there

01:09:35.080 --> 01:09:36.279
will be a major conference.

01:09:36.279 --> 01:09:39.330
This occurs every five or
six years, during which

01:09:39.330 --> 01:09:42.330
the very structure of
architectural education

01:09:42.330 --> 01:09:47.310
is going to be reassessed,
and perhaps, revised.

01:09:47.310 --> 01:09:49.830
And as we've started talking
about all of this stuff,

01:09:49.830 --> 01:09:52.380
I remind folks that
the last time we

01:09:52.380 --> 01:09:54.149
had one of these
conversations, which

01:09:54.149 --> 01:09:57.720
was only four or five
years ago, things like BIM,

01:09:57.720 --> 01:10:00.830
and integrated practice,
and Revit, and outsourcing,

01:10:00.830 --> 01:10:04.449
and work sharing were
not discussed at all.

01:10:04.449 --> 01:10:06.240
And yet we came up with
a set of guidelines

01:10:06.240 --> 01:10:09.130
for what architectural
accreditation should

01:10:09.130 --> 01:10:12.750
be without reference
to any of these things.

01:10:12.750 --> 01:10:15.960
So whatever we talk
about next year, which

01:10:15.960 --> 01:10:19.890
will set the standards for what
architectural education will

01:10:19.890 --> 01:10:24.900
be until 2015 is
almost certain to be

01:10:24.900 --> 01:10:27.930
a discussion about a number
of things, which none of us

01:10:27.930 --> 01:10:29.940
have begun to think
about in terms

01:10:29.940 --> 01:10:31.950
of what design
education will be,

01:10:31.950 --> 01:10:37.120
and how that will also be
affected by cultural, racial,

01:10:37.120 --> 01:10:40.310
and gender factors.

01:10:40.310 --> 01:10:44.000
I always like to ask what are
the skills that we really bring

01:10:44.000 --> 01:10:48.030
to the architectural process?

01:10:48.030 --> 01:10:51.029
What is the value added that any
architect brings to a client?

01:10:55.520 --> 01:10:58.930
How does one change a slide
if it doesn't want to change?

01:10:58.930 --> 01:11:02.350
And that is,
ultimately, knowledge

01:11:02.350 --> 01:11:04.420
of clients, and the
ability to listen,

01:11:04.420 --> 01:11:07.950
which is not something we
generally teach in school,

01:11:07.950 --> 01:11:10.690
needs and interpretation for
our clients, which some of us

01:11:10.690 --> 01:11:15.310
teach, problem analysis,
and problem solving,

01:11:15.310 --> 01:11:17.900
the ability to innovate,
which MIT has always

01:11:17.900 --> 01:11:19.090
been spectacularly good at.

01:11:19.090 --> 01:11:21.080
I always loved teaching here.

01:11:21.080 --> 01:11:22.990
Anytime I worked
out into a Carter,

01:11:22.990 --> 01:11:25.470
I felt as though between
the students and faculty,

01:11:25.470 --> 01:11:29.150
we were on the cutting edge
of knowledge in the universe,

01:11:29.150 --> 01:11:30.940
and I know that that continues.

01:11:30.940 --> 01:11:32.450
So that's not an issue here.

01:11:32.450 --> 01:11:34.362
And project management.

01:11:42.970 --> 01:11:44.520
And I have to go
through this list

01:11:44.520 --> 01:11:49.530
because as we talk
about globalization,

01:11:49.530 --> 01:11:54.895
and its impact on education
generally, there is a poll,

01:11:54.895 --> 01:11:56.980
and I think they
finally wrapped it up,

01:11:56.980 --> 01:12:02.080
that was done by the
European equivalent

01:12:02.080 --> 01:12:07.660
of the Association of American
Schools of Architecture.

01:12:07.660 --> 01:12:10.810
And what they asked was, what
is it that firms in Europe

01:12:10.810 --> 01:12:15.460
are looking for in
design school graduates?

01:12:15.460 --> 01:12:17.320
And it turns out
that what they found

01:12:17.320 --> 01:12:19.360
is very similar to
what the American

01:12:19.360 --> 01:12:22.570
Institute of Architects has
also found in its own polling.

01:12:22.570 --> 01:12:25.000
Number 10 on the
list is the ability

01:12:25.000 --> 01:12:27.850
to develop trans-disciplinary
understanding.

01:12:27.850 --> 01:12:31.390
Nine is the ability to learn.

01:12:31.390 --> 01:12:35.290
Eight is the ability
to evaluate evidence.

01:12:35.290 --> 01:12:38.830
Seven on the list
is design skill.

01:12:38.830 --> 01:12:41.530
Six is the ability
to apply knowledge

01:12:41.530 --> 01:12:45.040
that one has obtained
in school to practice.

01:12:45.040 --> 01:12:47.590
Five is the ability
to create designs

01:12:47.590 --> 01:12:50.470
that satisfy ascetic and
technical requirements.

01:12:50.470 --> 01:12:53.530
Four is the ability to work with
a high degree of both autonomy

01:12:53.530 --> 01:12:55.210
and collaboratively.

01:12:55.210 --> 01:12:58.240
Three is the ability to work
in interdisciplinary teams.

01:12:58.240 --> 01:13:02.800
Two, interestingly, are the
personal and social skills

01:13:02.800 --> 01:13:06.430
in expression that enable one
to interact with a client.

01:13:06.430 --> 01:13:10.030
And number one has nothing to do
with design as we traditionally

01:13:10.030 --> 01:13:10.960
understand it.

01:13:10.960 --> 01:13:15.250
Number one is the ability
to have critical thinking

01:13:15.250 --> 01:13:16.462
and analytical skill.

01:13:22.910 --> 01:13:24.350
Sorry to skip over
Robert Taylor,

01:13:24.350 --> 01:13:28.767
but you heard about him.

01:13:31.510 --> 01:13:33.370
We also need to
think about what it

01:13:33.370 --> 01:13:35.980
is that comes out of our
schools because there's a panel

01:13:35.980 --> 01:13:38.650
tomorrow that will be
talking about education,

01:13:38.650 --> 01:13:41.000
and how we go about assessing
quality in the schools.

01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:44.650
Obviously, great
faculty and resources,

01:13:44.650 --> 01:13:46.180
innovative academic depth.

01:13:46.180 --> 01:13:48.610
Again, not an issue here.

01:13:48.610 --> 01:13:51.640
Design within rigorous
curriculum, not an issue here.

01:13:51.640 --> 01:13:53.720
Excellent students,
not an issue here.

01:13:57.950 --> 01:13:59.770
But there are a
bunch of things that

01:13:59.770 --> 01:14:03.122
throughout this
contextualizing of what

01:14:03.122 --> 01:14:05.330
it is we're training for,
there are a bunch of things

01:14:05.330 --> 01:14:11.950
that we have to ask why
is it that people of color

01:14:11.950 --> 01:14:14.850
have been excluded from
all of this learning?

01:14:14.850 --> 01:14:17.050
What's going on?

01:14:17.050 --> 01:14:18.940
What is the problem here?

01:14:18.940 --> 01:14:21.220
And we've listed some
of the impediments,

01:14:21.220 --> 01:14:24.480
and we also know that there
have been many initiatives that

01:14:24.480 --> 01:14:29.350
have been put in place over
the course of the last 30, 40

01:14:29.350 --> 01:14:30.080
years.

01:14:30.080 --> 01:14:33.100
Ford Foundation invested
millions of dollars

01:14:33.100 --> 01:14:35.770
in conjunction with
the American Institute

01:14:35.770 --> 01:14:38.170
of Architects, starting
in the late 60s

01:14:38.170 --> 01:14:42.610
and into the early 70s, in
supporting scholarships.

01:14:42.610 --> 01:14:44.170
I can look around this room.

01:14:44.170 --> 01:14:47.560
I will do this because I
have to do this periodically.

01:14:47.560 --> 01:14:49.930
Are there any people
in this room who

01:14:49.930 --> 01:14:53.570
know anyone who got
one of those Ford AIA

01:14:53.570 --> 01:14:57.570
grads who became an architect?

01:14:57.570 --> 01:14:59.990
No.

01:14:59.990 --> 01:15:03.440
The point is that millions
have been invested

01:15:03.440 --> 01:15:06.380
in efforts and initiatives
that no one bothered

01:15:06.380 --> 01:15:10.080
to track the effectiveness of.

01:15:10.080 --> 01:15:14.250
So we did a lot of
feel good things,

01:15:14.250 --> 01:15:20.380
and those feel good things
got us from 1% to 1.5%,

01:15:20.380 --> 01:15:23.368
and that clearly hasn't
been good enough.

01:15:26.370 --> 01:15:28.560
So the recommendations
that have been put forward

01:15:28.560 --> 01:15:31.620
to try to change
all of this, include

01:15:31.620 --> 01:15:34.110
increasing public awareness
of what architecture is.

01:15:34.110 --> 01:15:38.450
I mean, let's face it, most
people have no idea what we do.

01:15:38.450 --> 01:15:40.460
Ensuring adequate
resources, that usually

01:15:40.460 --> 01:15:45.830
means scholarships, but it also
means other kinds of resources.

01:15:45.830 --> 01:15:50.380
Increasing the visibility of
architects of color, hence

01:15:50.380 --> 01:15:53.550
the images I've included here.

01:15:53.550 --> 01:15:56.790
And expanding the nature
and diversity of leaders

01:15:56.790 --> 01:15:59.940
within the profession,
growing people,

01:15:59.940 --> 01:16:02.580
and putting them out
front so that more people

01:16:02.580 --> 01:16:06.994
know about the work of Mel
Mitchell or Robert Colts.

01:16:10.410 --> 01:16:14.440
Improving licensure rates and
developing mentoring programs.

01:16:14.440 --> 01:16:17.370
And part of what we
realized is that there

01:16:17.370 --> 01:16:21.540
are not-- if there are only
1571 African-American architects

01:16:21.540 --> 01:16:26.010
in the United States, they
can't all be mentors as such.

01:16:26.010 --> 01:16:29.190
So it means we've got five
different kinds of mentors

01:16:29.190 --> 01:16:30.330
to step out.

01:16:30.330 --> 01:16:34.370
I am not, for example,
a licensed architect,

01:16:34.370 --> 01:16:38.160
and yet I've won the AIAs
Whitney Young award for my work

01:16:38.160 --> 01:16:41.640
in this field because I
believe in architecture.

01:16:41.640 --> 01:16:43.650
There are many other
people like myself

01:16:43.650 --> 01:16:46.870
who believe in architecture who
are not licensed architects,

01:16:46.870 --> 01:16:50.310
who are prepared to try to
work to change the numbers.

01:16:50.310 --> 01:16:53.250
We have to be called
upon, but the profession

01:16:53.250 --> 01:16:59.310
tends to have so much hubris
and insularity that it tends

01:16:59.310 --> 01:17:01.830
not to look to us very
often for what it is we're

01:17:01.830 --> 01:17:03.724
prepared to try to contribute.

01:17:06.660 --> 01:17:11.030
Requiring diversity
training within our firms,

01:17:11.030 --> 01:17:13.160
certainly, but
ordinarily, we try

01:17:13.160 --> 01:17:15.085
to start with our
local AIA components,

01:17:15.085 --> 01:17:16.460
where people just
sort of take it

01:17:16.460 --> 01:17:18.950
for granted that they're
so smart, and capable,

01:17:18.950 --> 01:17:22.010
and tolerant, and outgoing
that, in fact, they

01:17:22.010 --> 01:17:23.990
don't need to understand
what it is they're

01:17:23.990 --> 01:17:27.440
doing in their firms.

01:17:27.440 --> 01:17:30.650
Like the firm principal who
came to me once, and made

01:17:30.650 --> 01:17:34.260
a suggestion as to
what color of colleague

01:17:34.260 --> 01:17:36.080
he would like me
to-- like to see

01:17:36.080 --> 01:17:41.530
me showing up at
social events with as

01:17:41.530 --> 01:17:45.054
recently as four months ago.

01:17:45.054 --> 01:17:46.978
People just don't get it.

01:17:55.170 --> 01:17:58.540
And then finally, a set of
more specific recommendations

01:17:58.540 --> 01:18:01.840
that we're working to
develop that hopefully,

01:18:01.840 --> 01:18:04.760
people will talk about
here and elsewhere.

01:18:04.760 --> 01:18:07.840
One of the things we realize
is that by and large,

01:18:07.840 --> 01:18:09.550
the folks who go
into this field have

01:18:09.550 --> 01:18:12.490
been exposed to it
fairly early on,

01:18:12.490 --> 01:18:14.590
without regard to
race or gender.

01:18:14.590 --> 01:18:17.080
Someone in the family was
involved in construction,

01:18:17.080 --> 01:18:21.940
or had a hardware store, or
took him to Home Depot a lot

01:18:21.940 --> 01:18:24.564
to do minor repairs
around the house.

01:18:24.564 --> 01:18:26.230
It's something people
are familiar with,

01:18:26.230 --> 01:18:29.230
and we need to start earlier
than high school to begin

01:18:29.230 --> 01:18:31.540
to introduce folks to things.

01:18:31.540 --> 01:18:34.990
Corporate involvement of
design supply companies.

01:18:34.990 --> 01:18:36.880
It struck me that
in many respects,

01:18:36.880 --> 01:18:38.500
the easiest way to
do outreach would

01:18:38.500 --> 01:18:41.290
be to serve a card table
out in front of a Home

01:18:41.290 --> 01:18:43.960
Depot, or a Lowes in
a community of color,

01:18:43.960 --> 01:18:46.270
and have your schools sit
there, and say this is

01:18:46.270 --> 01:18:47.560
what it is to be an architect.

01:18:47.560 --> 01:18:49.660
We want you to think
about coming into this.

01:18:49.660 --> 01:18:52.540
Recruiting design
school faculty of color

01:18:52.540 --> 01:18:57.810
from fields other than from
the 1571 licensed architects.

01:18:57.810 --> 01:19:00.560
They can't maintain
practices at the same time

01:19:00.560 --> 01:19:02.440
that they're in a
classroom teaching.

01:19:02.440 --> 01:19:05.440
You're asking us to do too much.

01:19:05.440 --> 01:19:09.110
Giving voice to students
of color in design schools.

01:19:09.110 --> 01:19:12.220
I see one of my recent
graduates here, Brandi Brooks,

01:19:12.220 --> 01:19:17.390
who now runs the Community
Design Center of Boston.

01:19:17.390 --> 01:19:19.780
And when she was in
school, we saw to it

01:19:19.780 --> 01:19:25.060
that people knew who she
was, and all of our schools

01:19:25.060 --> 01:19:26.600
have to do that.

01:19:26.600 --> 01:19:30.250
Promoting a broad diversity of
opinions within private design

01:19:30.250 --> 01:19:33.920
firms, which doesn't
happen a lot these days.

01:19:33.920 --> 01:19:38.560
Monitor the government and
foundation diversity programs

01:19:38.560 --> 01:19:40.600
that exist at this
moment to raise

01:19:40.600 --> 01:19:42.400
the question of
whether they're just

01:19:42.400 --> 01:19:46.510
feel good efforts, or something
that really has an effect,

01:19:46.510 --> 01:19:50.800
and acknowledge and support
the cultural globalization

01:19:50.800 --> 01:19:51.910
of design practice.

01:19:51.910 --> 01:19:57.070
And that means recognizing that
while our schools and our firms

01:19:57.070 --> 01:20:02.440
are not necessarily up to speed
with the diversity of design,

01:20:02.440 --> 01:20:05.089
the reality is practice is.

01:20:09.720 --> 01:20:13.370
And finally, there is
a major effort that's

01:20:13.370 --> 01:20:14.640
being made at this moment.

01:20:14.640 --> 01:20:18.320
Finally, it's among the
architects organizations--

01:20:18.320 --> 01:20:20.990
to do what has happened
in law and medicine

01:20:20.990 --> 01:20:23.600
for the last 30
years, and that is

01:20:23.600 --> 01:20:27.560
to develop systematic data
tracking so that one knows

01:20:27.560 --> 01:20:32.500
who's enrolled in our schools,
what programs they're in,

01:20:32.500 --> 01:20:37.750
who graduates, who goes
into IDP, who takes the ARE,

01:20:37.750 --> 01:20:40.150
who becomes licensed,
where at this moment,

01:20:40.150 --> 01:20:42.940
again, there's no
centralized data source that

01:20:42.940 --> 01:20:45.040
enables us to know all of that.

01:20:45.040 --> 01:20:48.200
And literally, within
the last few weeks,

01:20:48.200 --> 01:20:50.140
the collateral
organizations that

01:20:50.140 --> 01:20:53.740
oversee architecture and
architectural education

01:20:53.740 --> 01:20:57.490
have finally begun to
ask those questions,

01:20:57.490 --> 01:21:00.790
and to develop a unified
data tracking system.

01:21:00.790 --> 01:21:03.010
And there are a
bunch of questions

01:21:03.010 --> 01:21:07.090
that we hope that this data
tracking system will finally

01:21:07.090 --> 01:21:08.318
help us to answer.

01:21:17.520 --> 01:21:19.890
So thanks.

01:21:19.890 --> 01:21:22.680
Yeah, I'm into the
global economy too.

01:21:22.680 --> 01:21:25.500
And I didn't get to
print all of these out,

01:21:25.500 --> 01:21:28.020
but I'll see to it
that we get these.

01:21:28.020 --> 01:21:29.790
Until about five
or eight years ago,

01:21:29.790 --> 01:21:33.690
there were not a lot of
useful sources of information

01:21:33.690 --> 01:21:39.240
on any of this, but the numbers
have grown fairly significantly

01:21:39.240 --> 01:21:41.600
in the last couple of
years, and since we're

01:21:41.600 --> 01:21:44.160
in an academic environment,
here are some good references

01:21:44.160 --> 01:21:49.130
for you, including Mel's
book and a couple of others

01:21:49.130 --> 01:21:50.830
done by folks who are here.

01:21:50.830 --> 01:21:55.290
So I'll say one
last thing, which

01:21:55.290 --> 01:21:58.050
is in most respects,
the hardest thing

01:21:58.050 --> 01:22:01.440
to say because you're not
supposed to know this.

01:22:04.380 --> 01:22:10.710
37% of our African-American
architects come from HBCUs.

01:22:13.300 --> 01:22:19.580
For the first time ever,
our accrediting agency

01:22:19.580 --> 01:22:23.030
has had to make the
difficult choice

01:22:23.030 --> 01:22:29.360
to discredit one of our
117 programs because

01:22:29.360 --> 01:22:33.570
of the weak performance
of that school.

01:22:33.570 --> 01:22:36.830
I know because of my position
that that school has received

01:22:36.830 --> 01:22:39.840
a letter, and that the
faculty at that school

01:22:39.840 --> 01:22:42.870
are now meeting with
their administrators

01:22:42.870 --> 01:22:46.800
to try to figure out what
should happen because there are

01:22:46.800 --> 01:22:50.090
only seven of those schools.

01:22:50.090 --> 01:22:52.760
So I can't tell you
who the school is

01:22:52.760 --> 01:22:56.100
because it's not appropriate.

01:22:56.100 --> 01:23:00.530
I will say that to the extent
that any one of our HBCUs

01:23:00.530 --> 01:23:05.570
fails that we have a
major problem in terms

01:23:05.570 --> 01:23:09.080
of increasing diversity
within the profession.

01:23:09.080 --> 01:23:15.290
And what is, for me,
most poignant and ironic

01:23:15.290 --> 01:23:20.890
is that that school that
has lost its accreditation

01:23:20.890 --> 01:23:24.880
is one of the schools that
Robert Taylor had the most

01:23:24.880 --> 01:23:25.720
contact with.

01:23:28.360 --> 01:23:32.530
Now, all of us, as
was the case at Tulane

01:23:32.530 --> 01:23:38.170
in New Orleans and the Gulf
region, all of us at schools

01:23:38.170 --> 01:23:41.590
figured out how to come
together after Katrina

01:23:41.590 --> 01:23:46.190
to provide support for
faculty and students

01:23:46.190 --> 01:23:51.290
who ran the risk of having their
academic careers fundamentally

01:23:51.290 --> 01:23:54.980
disrupted in the
post-Katrina era,

01:23:54.980 --> 01:23:58.550
and as a result of things
that all of our schools did,

01:23:58.550 --> 01:24:02.560
students who were at Tulane and
at other schools in that area,

01:24:02.560 --> 01:24:04.400
were able to complete
their studies

01:24:04.400 --> 01:24:09.980
and graduate because the rest
of us came together to help out.

01:24:09.980 --> 01:24:14.570
I would submit to you that over
the course of the next day,

01:24:14.570 --> 01:24:17.220
as you have your
conversations, one

01:24:17.220 --> 01:24:20.120
of the things you
need to think about

01:24:20.120 --> 01:24:24.320
is how we here in this room,
and in other schools that

01:24:24.320 --> 01:24:27.440
are doing well will
have to come together

01:24:27.440 --> 01:24:31.240
literally over the course of
the next two to three months

01:24:31.240 --> 01:24:35.080
to provide some aid and
support for one of our schools

01:24:35.080 --> 01:24:37.900
where because of the
lack of accreditation,

01:24:37.900 --> 01:24:40.510
students who
graduated last year,

01:24:40.510 --> 01:24:43.540
and students who are
graduating this year

01:24:43.540 --> 01:24:46.630
will not be eligible to take
a licensing exam because they

01:24:46.630 --> 01:24:50.860
will have graduated from a
non-NAV accredited school.

01:24:50.860 --> 01:24:54.190
So if we're going to talk
about doing anything,

01:24:54.190 --> 01:24:56.440
let us talk about
the help that we

01:24:56.440 --> 01:25:00.350
need to provide to those
schools and students who

01:25:00.350 --> 01:25:02.030
are most in need.

01:25:02.030 --> 01:25:04.530
And with that, you
have an overview,

01:25:04.530 --> 01:25:06.366
and let's have a
great conference.

01:25:06.366 --> 01:25:08.318
[APPLAUSE]

01:25:23.446 --> 01:25:31.417
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
third from the bottom.

01:25:34.209 --> 01:25:36.920
Good or bad, you just named--

01:25:36.920 --> 01:25:38.670
I don't know if it was
inadvertant or not,

01:25:38.670 --> 01:25:40.813
but you did name the school.

01:25:40.813 --> 01:25:43.218
LANDSMARK: I made
a reference to it.

01:25:43.218 --> 01:25:45.142
AUDIENCE: Yeah, you named it.

01:25:45.142 --> 01:25:48.553
Now, and I hope
this is something

01:25:48.553 --> 01:25:51.375
we're going to get a chance
to talk about tomorrow

01:25:51.375 --> 01:26:02.560
because the fundamental issues
as to why that school failed

01:26:02.560 --> 01:26:06.310
isn't anything anybody
at MIT did or didn't do.

01:26:06.310 --> 01:26:13.484
It goes to the heart of some
issues that we in our circles

01:26:13.484 --> 01:26:15.010
have to really,
really talk about.

01:26:15.010 --> 01:26:18.320
Whether this is the proper
venue or not, I don't know,

01:26:18.320 --> 01:26:22.615
but you get my drift here?

01:26:22.615 --> 01:26:24.830
LANDSMARK: Well, yeah.

01:26:24.830 --> 01:26:28.190
What we try to do when we
find out these kinds of things

01:26:28.190 --> 01:26:32.540
is to make sure that there
are appropriate offline

01:26:32.540 --> 01:26:36.080
venues for these
conversations, and I

01:26:36.080 --> 01:26:40.130
would anticipate that over the
course of the next day or so,

01:26:40.130 --> 01:26:44.046
there will be a number of
appropriate offline venues

01:26:44.046 --> 01:26:47.190
where this can be discussed.

01:26:47.190 --> 01:26:47.940
AUDIENCE: Got you.

01:26:50.880 --> 01:26:53.580
DAVIS: I know the focus
is an academic focus here,

01:26:53.580 --> 01:26:57.945
but in my experience
and practice is such

01:26:57.945 --> 01:27:02.980
that so much of the work
is in our profession is

01:27:02.980 --> 01:27:06.900
tied to a tradition that's
a gentleman's practice.

01:27:09.450 --> 01:27:12.534
So many of my colleagues
have learned--

01:27:12.534 --> 01:27:14.450
I learned about architecture
because my mother

01:27:14.450 --> 01:27:16.860
used to drive us around
as kids to look at houses,

01:27:16.860 --> 01:27:18.600
and that's how--

01:27:18.600 --> 01:27:23.450
but it was not because
of anything deliberate

01:27:23.450 --> 01:27:25.980
in the culture
that supported me.

01:27:25.980 --> 01:27:28.420
The person who built our
house was a black architect

01:27:28.420 --> 01:27:31.556
and builder not registered
in the state of Florida,

01:27:31.556 --> 01:27:33.972
but he build because he grew
up in a segregated community,

01:27:33.972 --> 01:27:36.390
and he had a clientele.

01:27:36.390 --> 01:27:40.200
Today, and as I was
growing up, most

01:27:40.200 --> 01:27:43.780
of the architects that
I knew were successful

01:27:43.780 --> 01:27:49.470
developed that success through
a clientele that was government,

01:27:49.470 --> 01:27:53.570
community based, some churches.

01:27:53.570 --> 01:27:56.150
Most of the architects that
I know who got their starts,

01:27:56.150 --> 01:27:58.131
got their starts in
the social fabric.

01:28:03.210 --> 01:28:08.050
I feel very strongly that
the schools have a role,

01:28:08.050 --> 01:28:10.760
but that role is
very, very limited

01:28:10.760 --> 01:28:14.720
in the sense that from a
practice point of view,

01:28:14.720 --> 01:28:17.950
the ability for us to begin to
strengthen the social fabric,

01:28:17.950 --> 01:28:21.190
and let's talk about
that more aggressively,

01:28:21.190 --> 01:28:25.840
and how we begin to get
people to think about

01:28:25.840 --> 01:28:31.520
in our communities, being
more aggressive about doing

01:28:31.520 --> 01:28:33.320
that is where it's at.

01:28:33.320 --> 01:28:35.500
I don't think the
problem of race

01:28:35.500 --> 01:28:38.280
are going to be solved
because white people decided

01:28:38.280 --> 01:28:39.841
to do the right thing.

01:28:39.841 --> 01:28:42.465
I think it's going to be solved
because white people understand

01:28:42.465 --> 01:28:46.300
that this culture will
not sustain itself

01:28:46.300 --> 01:28:51.200
without functioning in a
way that embraces all of us.

01:28:51.200 --> 01:28:55.250
And it's not going to be
solved because black people put

01:28:55.250 --> 01:28:56.930
pressure on white people.

01:28:56.930 --> 01:28:58.960
It's going to solved
because black people begin

01:28:58.960 --> 01:29:02.100
to think more aggressively
and creatively about how

01:29:02.100 --> 01:29:05.537
to provide a social structure
to build our own communities.

01:29:05.537 --> 01:29:07.120
LANDSMARK: It would
be helpful in that

01:29:07.120 --> 01:29:10.600
regard if you said who you are
and what your current work is.

01:29:10.600 --> 01:29:11.850
DAVIS: My name's Curtis Davis.

01:29:11.850 --> 01:29:14.200
I'm a [INAUDIBLE]
architect, but I've

01:29:14.200 --> 01:29:17.450
made a choice to be an
architect from the client side,

01:29:17.450 --> 01:29:21.480
and I work with the
Smithsonian Institution.

01:29:21.480 --> 01:29:23.670
LANDSMARK: Odd.

01:29:23.670 --> 01:29:24.170
[CHUCKLES]

01:29:24.170 --> 01:29:26.585
DAVIS: Why you going
to play me like that?

01:29:26.585 --> 01:29:27.990
[LAUGHTER]

01:29:27.990 --> 01:29:29.990
The National Museum for
African-American History

01:29:29.990 --> 01:29:30.680
and Culture.

01:29:30.680 --> 01:29:31.690
LANDSMARK: Thank you.

01:29:31.690 --> 01:29:33.190
DAVIS: But the point
that I'm trying

01:29:33.190 --> 01:29:35.920
to make about this
question of building,

01:29:35.920 --> 01:29:40.900
who builds, how we build
is what informs the school.

01:29:40.900 --> 01:29:43.762
I don't think that schools
inform the process.

01:29:43.762 --> 01:29:45.220
The schools ought
to be reflections

01:29:45.220 --> 01:29:48.200
of what the community
demands, and needs,

01:29:48.200 --> 01:29:51.110
and that's where the support
will come to the schools.

01:29:51.110 --> 01:29:55.570
And if the schools have a
role today in this transition,

01:29:55.570 --> 01:29:59.330
it's to raise that question,
and bring that debate

01:29:59.330 --> 01:30:03.470
to the table in a real,
meaningful, and aggressive way.

01:30:03.470 --> 01:30:06.880
LANDSMARK: One thing
I'll say about that

01:30:06.880 --> 01:30:08.710
is that it's been
interesting to me

01:30:08.710 --> 01:30:12.850
that more of that
consciousness has emerged

01:30:12.850 --> 01:30:16.120
from the students
themselves than from most

01:30:16.120 --> 01:30:18.210
of our faculties.

